window.DOCS_INDEX = [{"slug": "start", "title": "Get started", "section": "Start", "headings": [], "text": " Windage Engine is a Swift package. Add it, run the sample game that ships with it, then play that game by script and check it the way the engine checks itself. macOS 26+ Swift 6.2 Metal 4 GPU Add the package In your game's Package.swift : dependencies: .package(url: \"https://github.com/Pixla-app/WindageEngine.git\" , from: \"1.0.0\" ) Run the sample A complete small game — two levels, an editor, sound, a save — written against the public API only. It opens with the engine's splash; any key skips it and still does what it was pressed for. swift run WindageSample The ruins at eye height, drawn by the sample. Play it by script Keys and seconds, pressed through the same path a keyboard uses. The log says where the feet ended each segment. WINDAGE_SAMPLE_LEVEL=1 \\ WINDAGE_SAMPLE_WALK= \"d:0.7 w:1.0 w:0.4 wait:1\" swift run WindageSample WindageSample: walk 'w:1.0' → feet (4.20, 1.20, -0.00), highest 1.20, on the ground, eye 1.69 m up Check it Tests, every recorded walk, and every recorded frame — the three checks the engine runs on itself before a change is called done. swift test python3 Tools/walks.py python3 Tools/frames.py "}, {"slug": "sample", "title": "The sample game", "section": "Start", "headings": ["What is in it", "Run it", "Controls", "Environment variables", "How the code is split"], "text": " WindageSample is a complete, deliberately dull first-person game that ships in the engine's repository and is built against the engine's public API only. It exists to prove that someone who did not write the engine can build with it, and it is the best place to see how any feature is meant to be used. ! The courtyard, drawn by the sample (../images/courtyard.jpg \"The courtyard level.\") What is in it The game is a fetch quest: touch every marked crate before the clock runs out. Around that it carries everything a small game needs: - Two levels , COURTYARD and RUINS, shipped as JSON files in Sources/WindageSampleCore/Resources/ and loaded with Level.load(named:bundle:) . They differ on purpose: a mesh the courtyard never uses, a different spawn, nine objectives against six, and a sun of their own. Winning moves you on to the next. - An editor , built on the engine's LevelEditor , opened in place with Tab . - Sound : a pickup played at the crate, footsteps by surface, a brazier's crackle, all generated. With no output device the game runs quiet. - A save : best times per level through SaveStore , with a version field and a migration from the first format. - A settings screen for key and controller bindings, look sensitivity, dead zones, head bob and volume. It saves as you change it. - Animated figures that wave as you come near, shadows, lamps, particles and instanced debris. Run it No Xcode project, scheme or signing. The window opens on the engine's splash; any key skips it and still does what it was pressed for. The console prints which level file it loaded. Once the editor has saved, its copy lives in Application Support and shadows the file in the repository, so editing courtyard.json appears to do nothing. The console line says so, and ⌘⌫ in the editor throws the copy away. Controls The movement keys come from the player's bindings, so the on-screen hints are read from them rather than typed in. These are the defaults: Key Does --- --- W A S D move Left Shift sprint Space jump C crouch (slide when sprinting) click / Esc capture / release the mouse O settings P pause R restart the run N next level M cycle tone mapping: clamp, Reinhard, ACES Tab in and out of the editor With a controller connected, the left stick moves, the right stick looks and the menu button opens settings. In the editor: aim and click (or Space) to pick, arrows move, Q/E up and down, A/D turn, -/= scale, B copy, ⌫ delete, L lamp on and off, lamp height, , . lamp brightness, T choose a kind, I add one where the crosshair meets the ground, ⌘S save, ⌘Z undo, ⌘R redo, ⌘⌫ revert to the shipped level. Environment variables The sample reads its options from the environment, so a check or a screenshot can be repeated exactly. Variable Effect --- --- WINDAGE_SAMPLE_LEVEL=1 start on that level: 0 courtyard, 1 ruins WINDAGE_SAMPLE_CAMERA=x,y,z,yaw,pitch start the camera there (degrees) and fly instead of walk WINDAGE_SAMPLE_WALK=\"d:0.7 w:1.0 wait:1\" play a script of keys and seconds through the keyboard's own path; yaw: and pitch: turn the view WINDAGE_SAMPLE_RATE=60 simulation steps per second; default 120 WINDAGE_SAMPLE_CHECK=1 run as a check: no window shown, no keyboard, no sound, saves of its own WINDAGE_SAMPLE_FRAME= draw one frame to a PNG and quit WINDAGE_SAMPLE_SPLASH=1 or 0 show or hide the splash; by default it is off in a check or a frame WINDAGE_SAMPLE_SILENT=1 run with no audio engine at all WINDAGE_SAMPLE_FPS=1 print frames and steps per second, and the longest wait between steps WINDAGE_SAMPLE_CHARACTER=/path/Name load a rig from Name.usdc , its clips from Name_ .usdc beside it WINDAGE_SAMPLE_MEASURE_AUDIO=1 render the brazier's crackle offline, print its level at distances, quit A scripted walk prints where the feet ended each segment, which is what Tools/walks.py checks against its records. See walks and frames . How the code is split { split} The sample is three targets, and this is the shape the engine recommends for any game: Target Holds --- "}, {"slug": "first-game", "title": "Your first game", "section": "Start", "headings": ["The package", "A minimal GameLoop", "Getting it on screen", "What to watch for", "Input", "Where to go next"], "text": "A game is a Swift package that depends on the engine and supplies one object conforming to GameLoop . This page builds the smallest one that opens a window and draws something, using the same set-up the sample game uses. The package Three things in it are deliberate: - macOS 26 and tools 6.2 , which the engine requires. FrameRenderer also needs a GPU that supports Metal 4; its initializer returns nil otherwise. - .upToNextMinor . Before 1.0 a minor bump may break a build and a patch never does, so this gets fixes without surprises. - .defaultIsolation(MainActor.self) , matching the engine. GameLoop and FrameRenderer are main-actor types, and with the same default your game's code means the same thing on both sides. A relative-path dependency works too, for an engine checkout beside the game, but then the game no longer builds from its own checkout alone. A minimal GameLoop This game counts simulated seconds and draws the number with the engine's HUDRenderer . It draws no scene, so the frame shows clearColor . encodePrePasses is left out because it has a default that does nothing; encodeHUD has one too. Everything the GPU touches has to be in the renderer's residencySet , which is why the HUD's allocations are added and committed. The viewport is in pixels, and since that is what the HUD is begun with, HUD coordinates are pixels from the top left too — the text's position is its baseline. Getting it on screen The engine owns no window. A window, a view and a responder chain are your app's decisions, so main.swift makes them, exactly as the sample's does: Then: What to watch for - The view hosts the layer; it does not drive frames. CAMetalDisplayLink does. Leaving MetalKit's own timer running as well ( isPaused = false ) would ask for two frames of work per frame. - engine.game is weak. Something else has to keep the game alive. In main.swift the top-level let game does; in an app delegate, store it. - You own the display link and must invalidate it when you are done with it. - clearColor is radiance , tone mapped like the rest of the scene, so it will not arrive on screen as exactly those numbers. Interface colors drawn in encodeHUD do. - Shaders under swift run . SwiftPM's command line compiles no Metal. Ship your .metal source as a .copy resource and load it through ShaderLibrary.make(device:bundle:sourceNamed:) , which compiles the source at runtime when no compiled library exists. - Print unbuffered. print is fully buffered when stdout is not a terminal; the sample calls setbuf(stdout, nil) so a piped run shows anything at all. Input Keys reach the game through the view. The sample subclasses MTKView , overrides keyDown , keyUp , flagsChanged (Shift, Control, Option and Command arrive only there) and the mouse events, and hands them to an InputState , started once with start() . Poll it in beginFrame . See input . Where to go next - GameLoop — each requirement, frame slots and the order of a frame. - Fixed timestep — why step always gets the same delta, and how to draw between steps. - Camera and meshes — a scene to draw in encode . - The sample game — split into a window-free core and an app, which is the shape to copy as the game grows. "}, {"slug": "game-loop", "title": "GameLoop", "section": "The frame loop", "headings": ["The protocol", "What the engine owns", "The order of a frame", "Frame slots", "Pre-passes", "The interface after the scene", "Interpolation", "Threading"], "text": " GameLoop is what a game supplies to the engine's frame loop, and FrameRenderer is what calls it. The engine owns when things happen: pacing, frames in flight, the render passes. The game owns what happens: its pipelines, its uniforms, its draws. The protocol encodePrePasses and encodeHUD have default implementations that do nothing. The other four are yours. Set the game with engine.game = game ; the property is weak, so keep your own strong reference. Requirement Called Do here --- --- --- beginFrame() once per displayed frame, before any steps poll input step(deltaTime:) once per fixed simulation step, zero or more times a frame move everything viewportSizeChanged(width:height:) when the drawable's size, in pixels, changes, before that frame is encoded rebuild anything that depends on aspect ratio encodePrePasses(into:frameSlot:interpolation:) before the main pass shadow maps, depth prepasses, offscreen targets encode(into:frameSlot:interpolation:) in the scene pass the scene's draws encodeHUD(into:frameSlot:interpolation:) after tone mapping, in the same pass, with the same interpolation the interface What the engine owns FrameRenderer holds the Metal 4 objects every renderer needs: the device and a residencySet , and its own command queue and buffer, which it hands to your hooks rather than exposing. Everything the GPU touches must be in that set; add allocations as you create them, then call commitResidency() . init(device:simulationStepsPerSecond:) throws a FrameRendererError when the GPU is not Metal 4 or will not make what a frame needs. It also owns the targets. The scene is drawn into an HDR texture of FrameRenderer.sceneColorPixelFormat ( .rgba16Float ) with a depth attachment of FrameRenderer.depthPixelFormat , cleared to reversedZClearDepth (0, infinitely far). The engine then tone maps that into the drawable, of FrameRenderer.colorPixelFormat ( .bgra8Unorm_srgb ), and calls encodeHUD in that same pass. So: - Scene pipelines declare sceneColorPixelFormat and depthPixelFormat . - Interface pipelines declare colorPixelFormat and no depth. - Depth state comes from makeDepthStencilState() , which compares with .greater to match the engine's reversed-Z projection, simd_float4x4(perspectiveFovY:aspectRatio:nearZ:depthRange:) . A mismatch draws nothing at all rather than drawing badly, which is why the engine hands you the matching set. What untouched pixels show is clearColor , in radiance, and the exposure and curve between scene and screen are toneMapping . Both can change at any time. See tone mapping . The order of a frame For each frame the display link delivers: 1. The frame's time is credited to the clock. 2. beginFrame() . 3. step(deltaTime:) as many times as whole steps are due, each with the same delta. 4. The renderer waits until the GPU has finished with the oldest frame slot, then resets that slot's allocator. 5. encodePrePasses(into:frameSlot:interpolation:) , given the command buffer itself. 6. The scene pass: a barrier so it waits for what the pre-passes wrote, then encode(into:frameSlot:interpolation:) . 7. The tone map pass, then encodeHUD(into:frameSlot:interpolation:) . 8. Commit and present. viewportSizeChanged is called just before step 4 on any frame whose drawable is a new size, including the first. A pause is the game's to implement and costs the loop nothing: keep stepping, just don't advance. The frame loop keeps running, the scene still draws and the window still resizes. Frame slots { frame-slots} The CPU may run up to FrameRenderer.maxFramesInFlight frames (3) ahead of the GPU. frameSlot says which of those slots this frame owns, and the engine guarantees the GPU has finished with it. Anything the CPU writes per frame (uniforms, joint palettes, instance data) needs one copy per slot, indexed by frameSlot , or the CPU will overwrite numbers the GPU is still reading. The sample sizes its buffers that way: HUDRenderer.draw(into:frameSlot:) and the engine's other per-frame types take the slot "}, {"slug": "fixed-timestep", "title": "Fixed timestep", "section": "The frame loop", "headings": ["Why", "The type", "In FrameRenderer", "Drawing between steps", "Input belongs to frames, not steps", "Behaving the same at any step rate", "Choosing a rate"], "text": "The engine never hands step(deltaTime:) a variable delta. Wall-clock time accumulates in a FixedTimestep and is spent in identical steps, so a game behaves the same on a 60 Hz display, a 120 Hz one and a frame that stuttered. Drawing still happens as often as the display allows, between steps. Why A thrown object or a falling player is integrated step by step, and each step's result feeds the next. Integrate with each frame's own delta and the same throw lands somewhere different at 60 Hz than at 120 Hz, and somewhere different again on a frame that happened to stall. A miss caused by the machine being busy is not a game. With a fixed step, the simulation is a function of the inputs and the step count, and nothing else. The type Seconds are Double here because this is a clock that adds frame times up for as long as the game runs; what a step hands the simulation, step(deltaTime:) , is one short span and is Float , like the rest of the engine's per-step arithmetic. - advance(by:) adds one frame's elapsed time and returns how many whole steps are now due. Unspent time carries over to the next frame. A negative delta counts as zero; the clock never runs backwards. - maxFrameTime caps what one frame may add. Without it, a long stall (a breakpoint, a window drag) becomes a burst of steps that takes longer than a frame, which makes the next delta larger still. Time is deliberately lost instead: the game runs slow for an instant rather than spiraling. - interpolation is how far the display sits between the last completed step and the next, in 0.. The remaining floor is Float precision, not physics: an eight-second straight walk ends 0.1 mm from 48 m at 30 steps a second and 0.6 mm at 240. Choosing a rate 120 is the default and what the sample uses. A lower rate costs less CPU; a higher one samples fast events more finely. Pick once and keep it. If your own simulation code integrates anything nonlinear, test it at more than one rate, as the engine does, because a fixed step only removes the frame rate from the result, not the step size. When the window is hidden there are no frames, and so no steps unless you ask for them. See Running hidden . "}, {"slug": "running-hidden", "title": "Running hidden", "section": "The frame loop", "headings": ["Hidden is frozen, by default", "`runsWhileHidden`", "App Nap", "Drawing into a texture", "Headless"], "text": "By default a hidden game is frozen: macOS stops delivering display-link frames to a window nobody can see, and the simulation steps only when a frame arrives. This page covers the switch that keeps time without frames, and the call that draws a frame with no drawable at all. Hidden is frozen, by default When the window is hidden, minimized, covered or on another Space, its display link does not call at all. When the window returns, the first frame reports the whole absence and FixedTimestep.maxFrameTime throws away all but a quarter of a second of it. Measured: hidden for 10.2 s, one frame back reporting 10.23 s, 30 steps run for it. That is a fair pause for a single-player game. It is wrong for anything that has to keep time: a match with other players, a timed run, a scripted check that has to finish. runsWhileHidden { runs-while-hidden} FrameRenderer.runsWhileHidden is off by default. When on, a timer steps the simulation whenever the display link has been quiet for more than 0.1 s (three frames at its slowest rate): - The same fixed steps at the same rate, through step(deltaTime:) . - No frame drawn, and beginFrame() not called , because no frame begins. Anything a game polls in beginFrame (the controller, for instance) is not polled while hidden. If a hidden game needs it, poll in step too, as the sample does when it runs as a check. - One clock, credited once. A display-link frame is stamped with when it will be shown , some 50 ms ahead of the host clock, which is all a timer knows. The timer moves the frames' clock on by exactly what it credits, so no time is counted twice when the window comes back. - Time starts at the first tick when no frame ever comes. A window opened hidden, and perhaps never shown, still keeps time. The arithmetic is an internal type, FrameClock , tested apart from the timer and the display link. The sample turns it on only for a scripted walk, leaving ordinary play paused when hidden: App Nap { app-nap} macOS coalesces timers in an app it thinks is idle, and a hidden game with no sound playing looks idle. So while runsWhileHidden is on, the renderer holds a process activity with .userInitiatedAllowingIdleSystemSleep and .latencyCritical . Both are needed. Measured on a silent bundled app, hidden two minutes: Activity Longest wait between steps --- --- none 120 / 78 ms, ragged after a minute user-initiated only 165 / 54 ms user-initiated + latency-critical 33 / 32 ms The last row is as steady as an app playing sound. Idle sleep is still allowed, and the timer precision's power is paid only while a game asks for it. Steps per second cannot show App Nap. The hidden clock credits whatever time went by, so a timer slowed to once a second still runs 120 steps, in one burst. What shows it is the longest wall-clock wait between steps, which the sample prints with WINDAGE_SAMPLE_FPS=1 . Drawing into a texture { render-into} FrameRenderer.render(into:) draws one frame into a texture of your own instead of a window's drawable, with the same passes in the same order: pre-passes, the scene into its HDR target, the tone map and the interface. What comes out is what a player would have seen. It waits for the GPU , so the texture can be read the moment it returns. Use it for a frame compared against a recorded one, a thumbnail, or a saved photograph. The texture needs FrameRenderer.colorPixelFormat , .renderTarget usage and, to read it back, shared or managed storage. Like everything else the GPU touches, it goes in the residency set. From the sample's writeFrame(to:) : The bytes are BGRA. Swap red and blue to write an RGBA image. render(into:) does nothing if engine.game is not set. It informs the game of a new size through viewportSizeChanged the same way a displayed frame does; the sample also calls it directly so the aspect ratio is right before anything else. The sample draws its frame before any step , so the picture is identical on every run. Pass interpolation if you want a moment between steps. Headles"}, {"slug": "camera", "title": "Camera", "section": "The frame loop", "headings": ["The Camera type", "An eye, not a body", "Looking: mouse and stick are different quantities", "Reversed-Z, with an infinite far plane", "Three things must agree, so the engine owns them", "A slice of the depth buffer", "How the sample drives it", "Gotchas"], "text": " Camera is an eye in the world: a position in meters, a yaw and a pitch, and the view matrix that follows from them. Pair it with simd_float4x4(perspectiveFovY:aspectRatio:nearZ:depthRange:) for the projection, and you have everything a frame needs to know about where it is looking from. The Camera type Orientation is yaw and pitch with no roll. Pitch is clamped to Camera.pitchLimit , 89 degrees: at exactly 90 the look direction is parallel to world up, the right vector is undefined, and the image snaps. Yaw is kept wrapped to (-π, π by Float.wrappedAngle(_:) , because a session spinning steadily for an hour would otherwise reach angles a 32-bit float cannot resolve finely. Both hold however the angles are set — camera.pitch += recoil is clamped, camera.yaw += turned is wrapped. The basis comes from those two angles: Property What it is --- --- forward The look direction. right Horizontal only, so strafing never drifts up or down while looking at the sky. up right × forward . viewMatrix World space to view space. Right-handed: the camera looks down -Z , so anything in front of it has a negative view-space Z. An eye, not a body advance(_:deltaTime:) flies along the look direction, through anything. That is right for an editor, a photo mode, or a game that genuinely flies. A game with feet moves a CharacterController instead and puts the camera at its eye — see character-controller . Walking is along the ground plane, not the look direction, which is why the two are separate types. advance takes a MovementInput — axes rather than key names, so a stick or a replay can drive it as easily as a keyboard. Its direction is clamped to length 1, not normalized: two keys at once are not faster than one, and a stick pushed a third of the way goes a third as fast. Speeds are the camera's tuning.walkSpeed (6 m/s) and tuning.sprintSpeed (18 m/s). Looking: mouse and stick are different quantities There are two look methods, and mixing them up ruins the feel of one or the other. - look(mouseDelta:) is not scaled by time. A mouse delta is a displacement the player already made; scaling it by frame duration would make the same hand movement turn further on a slow frame. - turn(rate:deltaTime:) is scaled by time. A stick reports a held position, which is a rate: hold it twice as long and you turn twice as far. Sensitivities are tuning.lookSensitivity (0.0022 radians per unit of mouse movement) and tuning.stickLookSpeed (3.2 radians per second at full deflection) — your game's to change, per camera. A player's own sensitivity goes on top, through input . Reversed-Z, with an infinite far plane { reversed-z} fovY is the vertical field of view in radians; Float.radians(fromDegrees:) converts. This projection maps the near plane to depth 1 and infinite distance to depth 0 — the opposite of the conventional near-0, far-1. Depth is stored as floating point, and floats hold far more precision near zero, so the conventional mapping spends almost all of it in the first few meters and leaves the far field to z-fight. Reversed, the precision is where the distance is. The far plane is gone, not merely large: depth approaches 0 asymptotically, so there is no view distance to tune and nothing to clip against at range. One consequence shows up in culling : a Windage Engine frustum has five planes, not six. Three things must agree, so the engine owns them Reversed-Z needs the projection, the depth compare function and the depth clear value to agree, and a mismatch renders nothing at all rather than rendering badly. So: Piece Where it comes from --- --- The projection simd_float4x4(perspectiveFovY:aspectRatio:nearZ:depthRange:) Compare .greater (nearer is now larger) FrameRenderer.makeDepthStencilState() Clear to 0 FrameRenderer.reversedZClearDepth Use all three and they cannot disagree. Reversed-Z means larger is nearer . Any depth arithmetic you write yourself — a depth range, a bias, a comparison in a shader — has to read it that way round. A slice of the depth"}, {"slug": "meshes", "title": "Loading meshes", "section": "Rendering", "headings": ["Formats", "glTF and GLB", "The two load functions", "Errors", "The vertex descriptor and semantics", "Tangents for normal maps", "LoadedMesh", "BoundingBox", "AssetOrientation", "After loading"], "text": " MeshLoader turns a file into Metal meshes laid out exactly the way your render pipeline expects, along with each mesh's bounding box, its materials and its place in the file's node hierarchy. It reads whatever Model I/O reads, and glTF. Formats Model I/O reads USD, USDZ, OBJ, PLY, STL and Alembic natively, and the engine reads glTF 2.0 — .gltf and .glb — itself. FBX is not read : the only complete reader is Autodesk's SDK, which a public source repository cannot carry. Prefer USD or glTF for anything with materials or hierarchy; OBJ is fine for placeholder geometry. Emission, for one, reaches the engine from USD and glTF and not from OBJ. glTF and GLB { gltf} Pass a .gltf or .glb to either load function, exactly as you would a USD file. The engine reads it into an ImportedModel and hands that to Model I/O, so tangents, your vertex layout, bounds and materials come out the same way as for any other format. What is read: - Meshes from the default scene, each placed by its node's transform, which arrives as nodeTransform . Triangle strips and fans become triangle lists; points and lines are left out and reported. - Compressed files : quantized attributes ( KHR_mesh_quantization ), meshopt ( EXT_meshopt_compression , KHR_meshopt_compression ) and Draco ( KHR_draco_mesh_compression ). - Materials : the full metallic-roughness set with its textures, emission and KHR_materials_emissive_strength . Textures may be inside a .glb , in data URIs or in files beside a .gltf , as PNG, JPEG, WebP ( EXT_texture_webp ) or KTX2 ( KHR_texture_basisu ). KHR_texture_transform is baked into the texture coordinates. - Buffers embedded, in data URIs, or in a .bin beside the .gltf . A glTF texture lands the same way up as the same model's from USD: its coordinates are turned to Model I/O's v-up convention, so leave TextureOrigin at its default. Images that are not plain files — inside a .glb , in a data URI, KTX2 or WebP — are written once to the app's temporary folder, named by their contents, because a Material names its textures by URL. glTF's factors stay with their textures: a base color factor tints its texture, and a metallic factor of 0 keeps a metallic-roughness map from making a surface metal. What is not read: skins and animation . A skinned mesh arrives in its bind pose, placed where its joints stand at rest; load the rig from USD ). Cameras, lights and morph targets are ignored. A file that requires an extension the engine does not read is refused, naming the extension. Material cannot carry alpha mode and cutoff, double-sidedness, normal scale, occlusion strength, wrapping or filtering. To see them, or to write a model out again, read it yourself: ImportedModel is plain values — meshes, materials and images — in meters, Y up, with linear colors. A reader of your own, for a format the engine does not read, can make one and draw it with MeshLoader.load(model:named:device:vertexDescriptor:semantics:reportingTo:) . GLTFError says why a file was refused: unreadable , unsupportedVersion , unsupportedExtension or missingResource . Through MeshLoader , it arrives as the underlying error of conversionFailed . A sandboxed app can open a .bin beside a .gltf only when the two share a name — hero.gltf and hero.bin — and the app declares the .bin 's type with NSIsRelatedItemType in its Info.plist. Otherwise the read fails with missingResource . The two load functions The bundle form is a convenience that finds the file and calls the URL form. Its extension has no default: the extension is the format, and USD is the one to prefer. Use the URL form for anything a person chose — a file opened in an editor, a mod, an asset in a pipeline's output folder. Both return one LoadedMesh per mesh in the file. report receives warnings that are not failures, such as a mesh that cannot have tangents. Errors The vertex descriptor and semantics The descriptor you pass must be identical to the one your pipeline was compiled against. Model I/O will convert a file's layo"}, {"slug": "materials", "title": "Materials and textures", "section": "Rendering", "headings": ["Material", "What `MeshLoader` does for you", "TextureSlot and TextureSource", "Color against numbers", "Occlusion, roughness and metalness in one texture", "TextureStore", "The v axis", "Errors", "In the sample", "Normal mapping", "What things are made of, for sound"], "text": "Every LoadedMesh carries a Material per submesh, read from the file that named it. TextureStore turns those materials into GPU textures, loading each file once. Between them, a bought asset arrives with its surfaces intact instead of as untextured geometry. What a material says needs no GPU and is a plain, testable value; turning it into GPU memory is a separate, small class. Material This is the metallic-roughness parameter set , because it is the one everything speaks: glTF, USD's UsdPreviewSurface and every common exporter agree on it. The defaults — white, dielectric, roughness 0.65 — are chosen so a material nobody filled in still draws as a plausible painted surface. Each scalar is multiplied with its map when there is one, so a textured material normally carries a white base color and a scalar of 1. This is a material, not a material system. No shader graph, no permutations, no passes, no authoring model. Your shaders decide what the parameters mean; the engine only reads them faithfully. What MeshLoader does for you Material(_:name:) reads a Model I/O material, and it handles three things Model I/O gets wrong quietly: 1. Two properties can share a semantic. A textured UsdPreviewSurface diffuse arrives as both a float3 and a texture. Every property is read, and the first — the one the file authored — wins. 2. Model I/O invents a 0.18 gray base color for files that never mentioned one. A slot with a map takes a white tint instead, so a texture is not dimmed to 18%. 3. A map's scalar is set to the identity. A file that connects metallic to a texture supplies no scalar, and the default of 0 would multiply the map away. metallic , roughness and emissive become 1 when mapped. For OBJ, Model I/O always reports the same roughness whatever the file says. So when a material has a specular exponent ( Ns ), specularExponent is set and roughness is converted from it with the standard Blinn-Phong to GGX approximation, sqrt(sqrt(2 / (Ns + 2))) . TextureSlot and TextureSource Color against numbers TextureSlot.isColor is true for baseColor and emissive only. A color map is authored in sRGB and has to be decoded to linear on the way in. A roughness or normal map is not a color : its bytes are already numbers, and decoding it as sRGB bends every value — mid-gray roughness 0.5 arrives as 0.21, and a normal map's flat regions stop being flat. Both mistakes look like a lighting problem, so the slot decides, never the call site. Occlusion, roughness and metalness in one texture TextureSlot.packedChannel says which channel a packed slot reads: R occlusion, G roughness, B metalness — glTF's packing, and what exporters write. Model I/O does not pass on which channel a USD input reads, so the convention is assumed here. It also works for an unpacked grayscale map, whose green channel is its value. The sample binds one ORM texture per submesh, taken from the roughness slot, since all three slots name the same file and TextureStore loads it once. TextureStore - It caches by file, by color-or-data and by origin. Every crate naming the same map shares one texture. - Every texture gets mipmaps , because anything seen at an angle aliases without them. - Fallbacks let a shader always sample. white multiplies to nothing; flatNormal means \"no perturbation\". They differ on purpose: a white pixel decoded as a normal tilts every surface diagonally. - textures(for:) never throws . A missing map draws the object in its plain color and is reported once, rather than stopping a level from loading. - After loading, add allocations to the renderer's residency set and commit. A texture the GPU cannot reach is garbage that looks like a shader bug. The v axis Metal puts texture coordinate (0, 0) at an image's top-left; USD and OBJ put it at the bottom-left. TextureStore flips on load with .bottomLeft , the default and the convention of every format Model I/O reads. Use .topLeft for glTF-derived UVs or coordinates your game generates itself. Errors TextureStoreError h"}, {"slug": "shadows", "title": "Shadows", "section": "Rendering", "headings": ["The split", "Cascades for the sun", "The pass", "Batches", "Write uniforms before any pass", "Spot lights: ShadowAtlas", "Point lights: ShadowCubes"], "text": "Windage Engine draws three kinds of shadow: the sun's, in cascades; spot lights', in one atlas texture; and point lights', in a cube array. The engine owns the textures, the depth state, the bias and the encoder loops. Your game keeps the two things only it can know: its pipelines and its uniform layout. The split { split} The engine's Your game's --- --- the depth texture array and its per-cascade slices the pipelines , compiled against your shaders and vertex descriptor the reversed-Z depth state, front-face cull, slope-scaled bias the uniform layout , meaning the struct a model matrix sits in the per-cascade pass descriptors and the encoder loop the joint palette, where there is one the light matrices, per frame in flight the cascade numbers Cascades for the sun { cascades} ShadowCascades is a value you configure: where each cascade ends, the resolution, and how far the light box reaches. A courtyard and a long-range outdoor game need different splits, so the numbers belong to your game: That is the sample's set, for a courtyard seventy meters across (the defaults are a resolution of 2048 and a light depth range of 1500). Three behaviors are built in: - The light matrix snaps to whole texels , so shadow edges don't crawl as the camera moves. - Bias scales per cascade ( biasScales ). If a cascade covers 8× the world per texel, it needs about 8× the bias. - Each cascade fades into the next over the last blendFraction of its own span. Without that, you'd see a seam at a fixed distance from the player. normalizedDepthBias(forCascade:) derives the shader's bias from a texel's world size, so biasTexels doesn't depend on scene scale. Shaders get four-wide vectors: splitVector , blendStartVector , depthBiasVector and biasScales . The rasterizer's bias while recording is a ShadowBias — a constant and a slopeScale — given as rasterBias: (default .cascades ). Both parts are negative , because under reversed-Z, pushing an occluder away from the light means a smaller depth value. The atlas and the cubes take a rasterBias of their own, defaulting to .spot and .cube . The init checks its splits: one to ShadowCascades.maximumCount (four, the shader's slots), positive and increasing. Anything else stops there, rather than drawing a cascade nobody reads. The pass { pass} ShadowMap(device:cascades:) allocates the cascade array ( texture , in ShadowMap.depthPixelFormat ). Add its allocations to your residency set. It throws a ShadowMapError naming what the device would not create: the texture, the matrix buffer, the depth state or the argument table. ShadowAtlas and ShadowCubes below throw ShadowAtlasError and ShadowCubesError , with the same cases. Build each shadow pipeline from ShadowMap.pipelineDescriptor(vertexFunction:vertexDescriptor:label:) . It sets everything that has to be right: no fragment function, no color attachment and a raster sample count of one. You supply the vertex function and the vertex descriptor. Your shadow vertex function reads its inputs at the indices in ShadowBufferIndex — the same for all three passes: vertices (0), uniforms (1), lightViewProjection (2), jointPalette (3) and instances (4). A mismatch raises no error; it records the scene from the wrong matrix. Each frame, call updateMatrices once and use what it returns for both passes: For a view that isn't a Camera , such as an orbiting editor camera, use updateMatrices(frameSlot:cameraPosition:cameraForward:sunDirection:) . Then call encode(into:frameSlot:batches:) . Batches { batches} A ShadowBatch is one kind of geometry: a pipeline, plus closures that reach each object. The cascades, the atlas and the cubes all take the same list. A skinned mesh has a different vertex layout, so it needs its own batch. This is the sample's static batch: A skinned batch also passes jointPaletteAddress . bounds is optional; nil means \"draw everything\", and ShadowCubes uses it to cull. The second initializer, ShadowBatch(pipeline:encode:) , is for geometry that isn't one MTKM"}, {"slug": "lights", "title": "Clustered lights", "section": "Rendering", "headings": ["PunctualLight", "Clustering", "LightSet, and a budget that can't promise much", "Lights that belong to a placement", "Shadows for the lamps"], "text": "The sun and an environment map light an outdoor scene well, but they do nothing useful indoors: the sun doesn't reach inside, and sky-colored ambient lights a cellar as if the sky were overhead. A PunctualLight is a lamp. ClusteredLights lets each fragment find the few lamps that reach it, and LightSet checks that a level hasn't crowded too many into one place. PunctualLight { punctual} - color is color times brightness, unbounded. It is radiant intensity, not a paint color. A lamp is far brighter than white paper, and the HDR target and tone map handle that. - range is finite on purpose. Inverse-square falloff never reaches zero, so without a cutoff, every lamp in a building would count as relevant to every fragment. attenuation(at:) is inverse-square with a smooth window to zero at the range. The denominator has a floor, because a fragment exactly at the lamp would otherwise be NaN and draw black. - cone is nil for a point light. PunctualLight.Cone(direction:innerAngle:outerAngle:) takes half-angles in radians: full brightness inside the inner angle, nothing beyond the outer, and a smooth fall between. A single cutoff angle would read as a stencil rather than a lamp. A point light is just a spot with no cone, and the shader doesn't branch on it. Spot shading is saturate(cosAngle angleScale + angleOffset) , squared. A light without a cone uses a scale of 0 and an offset of 1, so the same expression comes out as 1 everywhere. The sample's lantern light and an uncollected crate's glow: Three helpers are useful when ranking lamps. influence(at:) is attenuation weighted by luminance. PunctualLight.luminance(_:) is Rec. 709 luminance. reaches(_:) tests a BoundingBox against a light's range and cone, and only ever errs on the side of saying yes. Clustering { clusters} Choosing a few lights per draw works only while draws are small. The sample's ground is a single 800-meter quad. With per-draw selection, that one draw got four lights for the whole level, and a spot five meters up lost to lamps standing closer to the plane. Clustering picks lights per fragment instead. ClusterGrid divides the view frustum into tiles across the screen and slices into the distance: - Slices are exponential. An even cut would make the near slices microscopic and the far ones hundreds of meters deep. sliceScale and sliceBias let a shader find its slice with one logarithm: slice = log(z) sliceScale + sliceBias . - lightRange is not a view distance. The engine's projection has an infinite far plane, but clustering needs a finite end. Geometry beyond lightRange still draws, it just gets no local lights. Choose the distance past which none of your lamps reaches. Rebuild ClusteredLights every frame. The grid is in view space, so it changes whenever the camera moves, even if the lights don't: It produces two arrays for your shader. ranges holds, for each cluster, an offset and a count into indices , which is a list of light indices grouped by cluster. Upload both, plus your own packed light array. The packed light layout belongs to your game, as does the shader that loops over a cluster's lights. maxLightsPerCluster is worth logging, because it sets the cost of the worst fragment. droppedIndexCount says how many entries did not fit in maximumIndices — zero in a frame that fits; anything else is a cell lit short. With 64 lights in a test scene, the busiest cluster held 9 and the average was 2.2. The sample's ruins have ten lights, and the busiest cluster holds 6. LightSet, and a budget that can't promise much { budget} LightSet(lights:budget:negligibleInfluence:) holds a level's lights. It defaults to a budget of 4 and a negligibleInfluence threshold of 0.02. affecting(_:) ranks the lights that reach a point or a box by influence, strongest first, and returns at most the budget. Ranking by influence instead of distance fixes the common problem: a light leaves the set at the point where it had nearly stopped mattering. It can't fix the general case. A fixed ligh"}, {"slug": "sky", "title": "Sky and environment", "section": "Rendering", "headings": ["ProceduralSky", "Radiance is not irradiance", "A level's sky", "EnvironmentMap", "Built on the CPU", "How the sample uses them"], "text": " ProceduralSky describes a sky as radiance arriving from each direction: a zenith, a horizon, a ground and a sun disc. EnvironmentMap prefilters that sky into two cube maps, so a shader can look up reflections and ambient light. Both come from the same source, so the sun you see, the sun that lights the scene and the sun that metal reflects stay the same sun. ProceduralSky { procedural-sky} A second initializer takes sunDirection: in place of the two angles. All values are linear radiance, not colors . The sun is thousands of times brighter than the sky around it, and the tone map brings that range down to something a display can show. - ground shouldn't be black. Surfaces facing down are lit by the ground. A black lower hemisphere is the most common reason an object lit by its environment looks like it's floating in a void. - sunAngularRadius is in radians. The real sun is about 0.00465. A wider disc gives a softer reflection. - sunPitchDegrees and sunYawDegrees read and write sunDirection as angles, which are easier to picture and adjust than a vector. ProceduralSky.direction(pitchDegrees:yawDegrees:) does the same conversion for any code that needs it. - sunDirection is normalized however it is set; a zero vector points straight up. - radiance(inDirection:) returns the radiance arriving from any direction, which doesn't need to be normalized. - matterLight is the light a white speck of dust scatters under this sky — see particles . Radiance is not irradiance { irradiance} sunRadiance is how bright the disc looks when you look straight at it. A surface facing the sun receives that radiance multiplied by the small solid angle the disc covers. Use sunIrradiance as your directional light's intensity , never sunRadiance . With the default 0.05-radian disc, the two differ by about 130×. Because the irradiance is derived from the disc, a wider sun comes out both softer and stronger. A level's sky { level} Level.sky is an optional ProceduralSky , saved as its own block in the level file . Every key is optional, so a block that names only sunPitch is valid. This is the sample's dusk ruins: Read it through Level.resolvedSky . That property also handles older files that set the sun with sunPitch and sunYaw in settings and have no sky block. With neither, it returns the default ProceduralSky() . An overcast level is darker and flatter overall, not just lit by a dimmer sun. The ruins' first sky kept bright blue-sky values and only dimmed the sun, and the stone washed out to near-white. EnvironmentMap { environment-map} It throws an EnvironmentMapError naming the cube the device would not create: .specularTexture or .irradianceTexture . Texture What it holds --- --- specular The sky convolved with the GGX lobe, one roughness per mip . Mip 0 is the sky itself, and the last mip is nearly uniform. Sample it at roughness Float(specularMipCount - 1) . irradiance The sky convolved with a cosine lobe: what a diffuse surface facing each direction receives. Sixteen texels a side is enough, because the result has almost no detail. Add allocations to your residency set. Building a map is a load-time cost, not a per-frame one: 92 ms in a release build and 2.6 s in a debug build. EnvironmentMap is nonisolated and Sendable , so a game that would rather not spend that on the main actor builds it in a Task.detached and hands the map back. Built on the CPU { cpu} The prefilter runs on the CPU deliberately. The parts that can be wrong are the cube face directions, the importance sampling and two convolutions, and as CPU code they have tests that need no GPU. Those tests caught three bugs: fireflies at high roughness, fixed by quadrupling the sample count per mip; an irradiance integral off by a factor of π; and a black lower hemisphere. One limitation is stated plainly: this prefilters a function , not a photograph. It can't load a captured HDR panorama. How the sample uses them { sample} The sample rebuilds its environment whenever a level loads, from th"}, {"slug": "tone-mapping", "title": "Tone mapping", "section": "Rendering", "headings": ["Why a curve is not optional", "The two passes", "ToneMapping", "Curves", "Two things worth knowing", "The curve in Swift", "The interface is drawn after tone mapping", "What is deliberately not here"], "text": "Shading doesn't stop at 1. Windage Engine draws the scene into a half-float HDR target, then a second pass applies an exposure and a curve to fit it onto the display. Your HUD is drawn in that second pass, after the curve, so its colors reach the screen exactly as specified. Why a curve is not optional { why} A sunlit surface with a specular highlight is genuinely brighter than white paper. Once ambient, direct and specular light are added together, values routinely go past 1.0. The sample's lit faces reach about 1.13 before anything shiny is involved. Written straight to an 8-bit display buffer, all of those values clip to the same white. The brightest part of the image ends up carrying no detail, and it reads as bad art rather than a missing step. The two passes { passes} FrameRenderer runs the frame as two passes: 1. The scene draws into FrameRenderer.sceneColorPixelFormat , which is rgba16Float . Half float holds far more range than a frame needs, at half the bandwidth of full float. It can't be memoryless, because the second pass reads it after the scene pass ends. 2. The tone map draws one full-screen triangle, generated from vertex_id with no vertex buffer. It reads the scene, applies exposure and the curve, and writes the drawable in FrameRenderer.colorPixelFormat ( bgra8Unorm_srgb ). GameLoop.encodeHUD(into:frameSlot:interpolation:) runs in this same pass. FrameRenderer.clearColor is in radiance, because it is cleared into the HDR target and tone-mapped along with everything else. A pipeline with the wrong pixel format draws static, not an error. Metal 4 builds pipelines separately from passes. A scene pipeline that still declares colorPixelFormat is accepted, and it writes 8-bit pixels into the 16-bit float target: magenta and blue noise, with nothing logged. Pipelines drawn in encode use sceneColorPixelFormat , pipelines drawn in encodeHUD use colorPixelFormat , and anything drawn into your own target uses that target's format. ToneMapping { tone-mapping} Set it on the renderer and change it whenever you like: Exposure is in stops : +1 doubles the light, and −1 halves it. Stops match how people think about brightness, and the useful range is small and symmetric, roughly −4 to +4. As a plain multiplier, that same range runs from 0.06 to 16. exposure gives you the linear multiplier when you need it. ToneMapping is Codable , as {\"curve\": \"aces\", \"exposureStops\": 0, \"whitePoint\": 4} with every key optional, so a game can keep its look in a file. The curve is written by name. Curves { curves} ToneMapping.Curve --- --- clamp Not really a curve. It's what a renderer does with no tone mapping, kept so you can see the difference. reinhard Extended Reinhard with a whitePoint . The gentlest curve and the most washed out. It scales all three channels by the same factor, so hue is exactly preserved. aces The default. Narkowicz's fit of the ACES filmic curve. It rolls highlights off gradually and lets very bright colors desaturate toward white, which reads as bright rather than clipped. ToneMapping.default is ACES at 0 stops. ToneMapping.off is clamp at 0 stops. If your game tone-maps in its own shader, set toneMapping = .off so the picture doesn't get a second curve. The sample cycles through all three curves on a key, using ToneMapping.Curve.allCases . It also adds the player's brightness setting to its own exposure: Two things worth knowing { gotchas} - ACES lifts the midtones. Middle gray 0.18 maps to 0.267. A scene whose values were picked before there was a curve comes out brighter once ACES is on. The sample sits at −0.5 stops for this reason. A game authored against the curve from the start would sit at zero. - The ACES fit isn't asymptotic. It passes 1 at an input of about 6.6, so the clamp afterward is doing real work. The curve in Swift { apply} apply(_:) is the definitive version of the curve; the shader transcribes it, and tests pin input–output pairs so the two can't drift silently. Your game can call it to pick an "}, {"slug": "particles", "title": "Particles", "section": "Rendering", "headings": ["ParticleRecipe", "Light or matter", "ParticleSystem", "ParticleEmitter", "ParticleRenderer"], "text": "Particles are the small things that show something just happened: sparks, embers, dust. ParticleSystem simulates them on the fixed step, with no GPU and no randomness that changes between runs. ParticleRenderer draws whatever is alive as camera-facing quads in one instanced draw. The sample uses them for embers rising off braziers and lanterns, a burst when a crate is collected, and dust when the player lands from a drop. ParticleRecipe { recipe} A recipe describes one kind of particle. Your game defines its own kinds, so the engine has no list of embers or smoke. Field Meaning --- --- count How many one burst throws. speed , spread Meters per second along the burst's axis and across it. lifetime , size Seconds and edge length in meters, before per-particle variation. growth Size growth over a lifetime: 0 stays the same size, 1 doubles. gravity Meters per second squared, signed : dust falls, an ember rises. drag Fraction of velocity lost per second. color Linear and unbounded. Values above 1 make a spark read as hot. opacity , fade Opacity at birth, and how it falls: (1 − t)^fade . glows Whether this is light or matter. Light or matter { glows} Matter (dust, smoke, dirt) covers whatever is behind it. Light (a spark, an ember) adds to it and covers nothing. Both go through one pipeline. Color is premultiplied, and a glowing particle writes zero coverage, which turns the \"over\" blend into an add. A dozen overlapping sparks get brighter, and a dozen dust motes get more opaque. For a spark, color is the light it gives off. For dust, it's an albedo : how much of the arriving light the dust passes on. The sample found this by landing: a 0.5-gray cloud on shaded ground came out as a white glow, brighter than the floor. A sky's matterLight gives the light a white speck of dust would scatter under that ProceduralSky . It is the sky's average radiance plus the sun's irradiance spread over 4π. Compute it once per level and pass it to draw . It ignores shadow, so dust beside a wall is lit as if it were in the open. ParticleSystem { system} Those are the sample's three recipes: embers, the pickup burst and landing dust. Two glow and one doesn't, so the single blend has to handle both. - burst(_:at:direction:seed:count:) throws a burst of the recipe at that index, along direction (up by default). count can be fewer than the recipe's count, never more. - step(deltaTime:) advances every live particle. Call it from your fixed step , so a burst plays out the same way at any frame rate. - particles , live , liveCount and appearance(of:) are for reading the state. appearance(of:) returns a particle's premultiplied color, its coverage and its current size. A Particle 's fields are read-only: the system owns the pool. A recipe's count and lifetime , and an emitter's rate , hold their limits however they are set — a lifetime is never below a millisecond, a count or rate never negative. The pool is fixed. When it's full, a dead slot is reused, and otherwise the oldest particle is recycled. Dropping the newest instead would silently throw away the burst that just happened, which is the one the player is looking at. There is no random() . Spread comes from an integer hash of the burst's seed and the particle's index, inside the engine. The seed should come from the simulation, never from a clock. The sample uses its step count: The hash used to be the shader one-liner fract(sin(x) × 43758.5453) . In 32-bit floats on the CPU, 300,000 draws gave only 8,923 distinct values. The integer hash gives 297,320. The landing dust only triggers on a real fall, not every step down a stair: ParticleEmitter { emitter} For a steady stream rather than a burst, use ParticleEmitter(recipe:rate:seedBase:) . rate is in particles per second. The emitter carries the leftover fraction from one step to the next, so twelve a second is twelve a second at any step rate. Give each emitter a different seedBase so two emitters don't throw identical particles. The sample keeps one em"}, {"slug": "instancing", "title": "Many copies", "section": "Rendering", "headings": ["InstanceBatches", "Order is fixed, on purpose", "InstanceBuffer", "The shader contract", "The sample's debris", "Shadows for instanced copies", "What it does not do"], "text": "A wood is a few hundred trees of two or three kinds; a rubble field is a hundred stones of one. Drawn as separate objects, that is a draw call per copy per submesh, and the CPU cost grows with every copy. Drawn instanced, it is one draw per submesh per kind , and the copies are a buffer of transforms the GPU indexes. Windage Engine splits this in two, the way it splits particles: InstanceBatches groups copies on the CPU and is tested without a GPU; InstanceBuffer puts the result where a shader can read it. InstanceBatches Hand it (key, transform) pairs. The key is whatever names a kind in your game — a mesh name, an index into your list of loaded meshes. The result is every copy's transform in one array, kind after kind, and a Batch per kind saying where its run starts and how long it is. Order is fixed, on purpose Kinds come in the order they were first seen, and copies in the order they were given. A dictionary's order would change between runs, and with it which kind is drawn first — invisible in an opaque pass, and a flicker the day a transparent kind is instanced. The same input gives the same draws every run. InstanceBuffer write(_:frameSlot:) copies a frame's transforms into a buffer and returns it, or nil when there are none. - One buffer per frame in flight. A frame still being drawn is reading the previous one; rewriting it in place would draw a copy at the next frame's position for a frame. - Grown, never shrunk , with room to spare: half as much again as needed, never below 1,024 copies. A scene that keeps adding copies does not reallocate every frame. The rule is tested. - Residency is handled. A grown buffer is added to the renderer's residency set and committed, and the old one is removed. The engine draws nothing here. Your vertex function reads the copies, because the vertices are yours. The shader contract One float4x4 per copy, starting at the batch's byteOffset , indexed by instance_id , applied on top of whatever per-object transform you already have. Bind the buffer's address plus byteOffset , then draw each submesh with instanceCount: batch.count . The sample's instanced vertex function: Buffer 7 is the sample's choice for the main pass. Only the shadow pass's index is fixed by the engine. The sample's debris SampleRubble.transforms(around:count:margin:seed:) scatters small boxes among a level's buildings — deterministic, so the recorded frames never drift — and the sample groups them as one kind: Each frame, before any pass, it writes them: Writing before the shadow passes means the shadows and the scene draw the same copies. Then the main pass: Every piece is evenly sized , so the shader can turn normals with the copy's own rotation. A copy squashed along one axis would need its inverse transpose, per copy. Shadows for instanced copies ShadowMap normally draws object by object. Copies are not objects, so they go in a batch that encodes its own draws : The engine binds the pipeline and the light matrix, then calls the closure once per sun cascade — and once per atlas tile and cube face for lamp shadows. Your closure binds what its vertex function reads and draws. The copies go at ShadowBufferIndex.instances (4): and the shadow vertex function reads them there: The engine cannot cull a self-drawing batch per object, so it hands you a ShadowPass with the pass's target — which cascade, tile or cube face — and its viewProjection . The sample's debris is small enough to ignore it; a wood should build a Frustum from it and draw only the copies that pass can see — see culling . See shadows for the rest of the pass. What it does not do - Pick detail by distance. That is MeshDetail — see level-of-detail . The sample's scenes are too small to need it. - Cull for you. Which copies go into a frame is your game's choice. - Own the vertex function. The contract is the buffer layout; the shader is yours. "}, {"slug": "level-of-detail", "title": "Level of detail", "section": "Rendering", "headings": ["Tiers", "The steps, for a mesh that did not come from MeshLoader", "Choosing a tier: the half-pixel rule", "Recomputed normals", "Permissive simplification", "Drawing a tier is the game's job", "What it does not do", "The license you ship"], "text": " MeshDetail makes simpler versions of a mesh when it loads, and gives you the rule that picks one for how far away a copy stands. You need it when a scene has thousands of copies of a heavy mesh, most of whose triangles are detail too small to see from where they stand. Tiers { tiers} A mesh gets up to ten tiers ( MeshDetail.Options.maximumTiers ), each aiming for about half the triangles of the one before ( reduction ). Tier 0 is the mesh as loaded. They are called tiers rather than levels because in this engine a level is a Level file. Tiers stop early when a tier would keep more than stall (85%) of the triangles before it, and a submesh of smallestTriangleCount (12) triangles or fewer is left alone. Those are the defaults of MeshDetail.Options ; pass your own as options: when your meshes ask for different ones. For a mesh that came through MeshLoader , one call does everything: vertexDescriptor is the one the mesh was loaded with. Attribute positionAttribute is where each vertex's position is read, and normalAttribute where the normal each tier rewrites goes; both are three floats in the same buffer, whose layout gives the stride. The mesh's own buffers are read on the CPU. A mesh with a submesh that is not triangles gets no tiers. Simplifying a 19,000-triangle mesh takes a few milliseconds. Each MeshDetail.Tier has: Property What it is --- --- vertices UInt8 in the full mesh's vertex layout, three vertices per triangle submeshes UInt32 , per submesh, indices into vertices error the worst of its submeshes' errors, in the mesh's units Tier i is reduction i of every submesh, so a copy changes all its parts at once. A submesh with fewer reductions repeats its coarsest. The steps, for a mesh that did not come from MeshLoader The call above is two public steps. reductions(indices:positions:vertexCount:stride:) simplifies one submesh into MeshDetail.Reduction , each with indices into the full mesh's vertices and an error ; positions points at the first vertex's three position floats. tiers(_:full:vertices:stride:positionOffset:normalOffset:) turns per-submesh reductions into tiers; full is each submesh's own indices, and vertices points at the first vertex itself. Both take options: . Every reduction is made from the full mesh, so errors do not add up from tier to tier. Choosing a tier: the half-pixel rule { choose} Each tier's error is how far its surface strays from the original, in the mesh's units. Seen from far enough away, that covers less than half a pixel and the tier cannot be told from the full mesh. So there is no table of distances to tune per asset. - errors i is tier i + 1 's error. A result of 0 means the full mesh; a result of n means tiers n - 1 , because the array tiers(for:…) returns starts below the full mesh. - distance is from the eye to the nearest the copy can come: its box's center less its radius. A distance of 0 or less returns the full mesh. - scale is the copy's size. A tree scaled up strays further. - pixelsPerRadian is the viewport's height in pixels over its vertical field of view in radians. - maximumPixelError defaults to 0.5 . Half a pixel, not one. At one pixel, a wood of trees six pixels tall visibly lost their tiers of branches: a pixel of error all round an outline that small adds up to a different shape. The tests check that a farther copy never gets a finer tier than a nearer one. Recomputed normals { normals} A simplified mesh keeps some vertices and drops the rest, and a kept vertex keeps its normal. On a faceted prop that normal belonged to one face of many, perhaps one facing the ground, so a far copy drew nearly black. Every tier therefore gets its own vertices, with normals made again from its own faces : each corner's normal is the area-weighted average of the faces meeting at its position that bend less than 60° from its own face ( MeshDetail.Options.creaseCosine ), so hard edges stay hard. The cost is three vertices per triangle of each tier, which halves with every tier: about 1 "}, {"slug": "culling", "title": "Culling", "section": "Rendering", "headings": ["Why cull an open scene", "Frustum", "Five planes, not six", "Reversed-Z, the right way round", "The asymmetry behind every detail", "BoundingBox, in world space", "Culling shadow passes", "A batch that draws itself culls itself", "What it does not do"], "text": " Frustum takes a view-projection matrix and answers one question: could any part of this box be visible? Use it to skip drawing what is behind the player or off to the side, and to skip shadow casters a light's pass cannot see. Why cull an open scene A 70° field of view is under a fifth of the circle. In a scene of a few hundred objects, most of them sit behind the player's shoulders whether or not anything blocks them, so testing each against the view is cheap and saves a draw call per object. It matters more for shadows, where the same scene is recorded several times from a light's point of view. Frustum Build it from the same world-to-clip matrix you draw with, and test world-space boxes against it: Each plane is a (normal, distance) pair, unit-normalized and pointing inward: a point is inside when dot(normal, point) + distance = 0 for every plane. Five planes, not six The engine's projection, simd_float4x4(perspectiveFovY:aspectRatio:nearZ:depthRange:) , has an infinite far plane ). Extract the far plane from it and you get a zero normal, because there is no far plane to extract. Frustum drops it, so a Windage Engine frustum has five planes: left, right, bottom, top and near. Code written expecting six is written for a different projection. It would either carry a plane that always passes or divide by zero normalizing it. The tests check for exactly five unit-length planes, and that a box 100 km down the view is still visible. Reversed-Z, the right way round The planes are extracted from the matrix rows (Gribb–Hartmann). The textbook form assumes near maps to depth 0 and far to w ; under reversed-Z near maps to 1 and far to 0, so the near and far rows swap. Frustum does this for you. Extracted the textbook way, the near plane would cull everything close to the camera — which reads as geometry failing to load, not as a culling bug. The asymmetry behind every detail A false positive costs a draw call. A false negative deletes something the player can see. Those are not comparable, so mayIntersect leans toward keeping: - It is the standard conservative box test. For each plane it takes the box corner furthest along the plane's normal and rejects only if even that corner is outside. - A box straddling a plane is kept, even when most of it is outside. That costs a draw, never a missing object. BoundingBox, in world space mayIntersect takes a BoundingBox in world space. A LoadedMesh 's bounds are in the mesh's own space, so transform them first. BoundingBox.transformed(by:) re-derives the box from all eight corners, which stays correct under rotation. A Volume does this for you: Remember the mesh's nodeTransform belongs inside the transform — see meshes . A skinned mesh's bounds are its bind pose. An arm raised at runtime leaves the box, and a culling test against it deletes the character's shadow at exactly the moment it moves. Widen it with expanded(by:) ; the sample uses half a meter for its figures: Culling shadow passes A shadow is the scene drawn again from a light's point of view, so the same test applies per pass. A ShadowBatch of objects takes an optional bounds closure — this drawable's world-space box — for the passes that can use it: nil means draw everything, which is the conservative direction. With bounds supplied: Pass What it culls --- --- ShadowCubes (point lights) Casters outside the light's range skip all six faces; casters outside a face's Frustum skip that face. ShadowMap (sun cascades) Ordinary batches are drawn whole: a cascade covers what the camera covers, so there is little to remove. See shadows and lights . A batch that draws itself culls itself A batch built with ShadowBatch(pipeline:encode:) — instanced copies, or geometry your game builds into its own buffers — encodes its own draws, so the engine cannot cull it per object. Instead, the closure is told which pass it is drawing: Build a Frustum from pass.viewProjection and draw only what it can see: A sun cascade near the eye covers only a few mete"}, {"slug": "hud", "title": "The HUD", "section": "Rendering", "headings": ["Why it draws after tone mapping", "Making one", "A frame", "What it draws", "Polygons and feather", "Text", "FontAtlas", "InterfaceColor", "Limits"], "text": " HUDRenderer draws your game's interface: text, filled and outlined rectangles, lines, rings and polygons, in screen space, after the scene has been tone mapped. Use it for a reticle, a timer, a bar, a panel, a settings screen or a credit. It is one pipeline and one draw call for the whole interface. Why it draws after tone mapping { order} Scene colors are radiance: they pass through an exposure and a filmic curve on the way to the screen, by design. An interface color is a specification, and a brand color must land on screen as exactly that color. So FrameRenderer calls your GameLoop 's encodeHUD(into:frameSlot:interpolation:) after the scene and its tone map, and the HUD's pipeline applies no curve. The ordering is structural, not something each game has to remember. encodeHUD has a default implementation that does nothing; implement it to draw an interface. See tone mapping for what happens to the scene before it. Making one { init} The initializer is init(device:font:additionalCharacters:maxVerticesPerFrame:) and throws a HUDRendererError saying whether the font atlas, the pipeline or a buffer could not be built. Its allocations (the vertex buffer and the glyph texture) must be in the residency set, like everything else the GPU reads. A frame { frame} From the sample's encodeHUD , trimmed: begin(viewportSize:) clears last frame's shapes and sets the coordinate space. Every position is a SIMD2 with the origin at the top left , in whatever units you pass as the viewport. The sample passes the drawable's size in pixels and scales against a 1080-pixel reference, so text is the same share of the screen on any display. Raw pixel sizes on a Retina display come out at half the size you meant. draw(into:frameSlot:) uploads and draws everything since begin , in the order it was added: later shapes draw over earlier ones. frameSlot must be the one encodeHUD was given. What it draws { shapes} Method Draws --- --- fillRect(origin:size:color:) a filled rectangle, origin its top-left corner strokeRect(origin:size:thickness:color:) an unfilled rectangle, as four edges line(from:to:thickness:color:) a line of a thickness, as a rotated quad fillRing(center:innerRadius:outerRadius:segments:color:) a filled ring; segments defaults to 96 fillPolygon(_:color:feather:) a filled polygon, concave or convex text(_:atBaseline:pointSize:color:) a line of text; returns its width width(of:pointSize:) a string's width without drawing it A ring with a large outer radius is a circular vignette, which is how a scope's surround is drawn. Polygons and feather fillPolygon(_:color:feather:) takes an outline in order, either way round, and triangulates it by ear clipping, so concave shapes such as a letter fill correctly. The outline must be simple, with no edge crossing another; one that is not is drawn only in part. The HUD pipeline has no multisampling, so a filled shape with a diagonal edge would be stair-stepped. feather (default 1 , in the HUD's own units, so a pixel when the viewport is in pixels) runs a strip around the outline that fades from the color to nothing. Pass 0 for hard edges, for a shape that must tile exactly against its neighbor. The engine's mark is drawn this way. Text { text} atBaseline is the baseline, not the top — hence the label. Glyphs draw upward from it. Pass a top margin as its y and the first line's ascenders run off the top of the screen. Put the first line at least one pointSize below where text should start. Baselines make two readouts line up even when one has a descender or a comma. text returns the width it drew, which SplashScreen uses to set letters one at a time with extra spacing; width(of:pointSize:) measures without drawing, for centering or right-aligning, and the atlas's lineHeight(pointSize:) gives the distance from one baseline to the next. FontAtlas Glyphs come from a FontAtlas , rasterized at launch from an installed font: init(device:font:additionalCharacters:) , which throws a FontAtlasError when the charac"}, {"slug": "skeletons", "title": "Skeletons and clips", "section": "Characters", "headings": ["The types", "Loading a rig", "Posing a character", "What a pose gives you", "Scale and orientation", "Gotchas", "What it does not do"], "text": "A skinned character is a mesh, the skeleton it was bound to, and clips that move the skeleton. RigLoader reads the skeleton and clips from USD, SkeletonPose poses them, and you get the matrices a vertex shader skins with. None of it needs a GPU, so all of it runs under swift test . The types Type What it is --- --- Skeleton Structure only: joint paths, derived parentage, the rest pose and the inverse bind matrices. Loaded once, shared by every character wearing the rig. AnimationClip Keys for translation, rotation and scale, with the clip's own joint names. Each channel is a JointTrack . Both check their data when made, and throw an AnimationClipError for keys of the wrong shape. AnimationBinding Which of a rig's joints each of a clip's joints drives, matched by path, once . A playhead makes it; unboundJoints and isEmpty are what you read. AnimationPlayhead A clip, its binding to a rig, and the time. SkeletonPose A rig with a pose on it, one per character, kept between frames. Rig What RigLoader returns: a skeleton , its clips and a nodeTransform . Loading a rig RigLoader.load(named:withExtension:bundle:) reads the skeleton and every clip from one file; load(url:) does the same from anywhere on disk. The extension and the bundle are always named: .main is right for an app and wrong for a SwiftPM target, whose resources are in .module . A real character usually arrives as one file per clip, and each of those files carries a whole copy of the mesh and the skeleton. Take the rig from the base file and only the clips from the others: load(url:clipURLs:reportingTo:) does the same from disk. The closure is handed a RigLoader.Warning — .noAnimation , .clipDrivesNoJoints or .clipFileFailed — each printing as a sentence. Both: - Rename a file's only clip to the file's name , so a game asks for figure-wave , not the exporter's name. - Drop and report a clip that drives none of the rig's joints — otherwise it looks exactly like a character standing still. - Report a clip file that is missing or unreadable, and load the rest. Only the base file failing throws. - Ignore the clip files' skeletons. Their rest pose is wherever the exporter left the rig; merged, every joint a clip does not animate stands wrong. RigLoader.clips(named:withExtension:bundle:) and clips(url:) read only animations; Rig.adding(_:) puts them on a rig. rig.joints(notDrivenBy: clip) lists joints a clip ignores: empty is what you want. Loading throws a RigLoaderError — .assetNotFound (by name or by URL), .noSkeleton , .conflictingGeometryBindTransforms , .malformedClip — or a SkeletonError for a missing or out-of-order parent or a bind transform that cannot be inverted, rather than drawing a limb in the wrong place. Posing a character Make a pose and one playhead per clip when the character is placed: A new pose starts at rest. A playhead loops by default; looping: false makes a one-shot that clamps and reports isFinished . Its time may run past duration . Each frame, advance the playheads and pose the rig. One clip is pose.set(to: idle) . Two is a cross-fade, from the sample: weight is how much of the second clip, 0 to 1. Each clip is sampled at its own time. Ease the weight rather than switching it. Posing allocates nothing. A playhead must be made with the pose's skeleton, or one with the same joints in the same order; one made for another rig stops with a message naming the clip, rather than posing garbage. For anything that is not a clip — a head turn, a solver — use set(to:) with an array of JointTransform s, one per joint, which keep rotation as a quaternion because a matrix cannot be blended. What a pose gives you Property Space For --- --- --- localTransforms joint-local what a clip writes, and where blending happens modelTransforms the character's own space attaching things to a joint skinningMatrices model, relative to the bind what the vertex shader multiplies by skinningMatrices is already exactly what a shader indexes, so it is copied into a buffer as it is"}, {"slug": "character-controller", "title": "CharacterController", "section": "Characters", "headings": ["One step", "The world it stands in", "Footprints", "Things that move", "Tuning", "Crouch, slide and room to stand", "What goes on the view, never the aim", "The same at any step rate", "What it does not do"], "text": " CharacterController is a body with feet. It walks, stands on things, steps up a low ledge, falls off a high one, jumps, crouches and slides. For an editor, a photo mode or something that flies, move a Camera with advance(_:deltaTime:) instead. One step Step it once per fixed timestep and put the camera at its eye. From the sample: Only the yaw goes in : walking is along the ground plane, so looking down does not slow you. input is a MovementInput from InputState.movement ). isJumpHeld is whether jump is held ; the controller finds the press itself. To put the body somewhere else — a respawn, the next level — teleport(to:) it: everything it was doing is dropped, as a fresh controller would have it, and its tuning is kept. After a step, read: Property What it is --- --- feet where the feet are; collision is exact against this eye where the camera goes: the feet, the posture and the eye's settling isGrounded , verticalVelocity on something, and how fast up isSliding , slideProgress a slide, 0 at entry to 1 at the end eyeHeight feet to eye for the current posture, between crouched and standing isCrouchForced let go of crouch with no room to stand groundSolid which solid the feet are on, or nil for terrain or air events .stride(foot:speed:) , .landed(impactSpeed:) , .jumped — cleared at the start of every step The sample plays footsteps from events , picking the sound by groundSolid . The world it stands in Surroundings is a terrain height and a list of Solid s — each a box, and what the body needs to know about it. Terrain is a floor you cannot go below; walking into a rise walks up it. A solid is stood on, stepped onto if low enough, and otherwise stops you. The body is a box, which is exact against the axis-aligned boxes Volume.worldBounds produces; it moves one axis at a time, so a wall hit at an angle is slid along. Footprints A turned crate's world box is bigger than the crate. Give a solid its real plan in footprint (nil means the box): Footprint(of:) for a volume turned about the vertical (nil if tilted), Footprint(discOf:) for something round — a box cannot say it holds a circle — or Footprint(center:halfSize:yaw:) and Footprint(center:radius:) by hand. A footprint is fixed once made; a new plan is a new footprint. The box says how high a solid reaches; the footprint says where it stands. Things that move Moving a solid is your game's job: update its bounds and say how far it went. Keep the order of solids fixed — the body knows what it stands on by index. - motion — how far the solid moved this step. A body on one goes with it, through collision, so a platform carries no one through a wall. - turn — a Turn(about:by:) : the axis's center and the angle turned this step, in radians. Nil for none. A solid moving into the body pushes it; pushed into a wall, pinnedBy names the solid, and what it does next is your game's. On leaving, the body keeps the speed it was carried or pushed at ( groundVelocity , airVelocity ), so motion must be movement : a solid reset to its start must report none that step. The camera is your game's. Add turned to the view's yaw, or the floor spins and the player keeps facing one way. Tuning tuning holds every number; the defaults were tuned in play. Field Default Why --- --- --- walkSpeed , sprintSpeed 6, 18 m/s a jog and a run, at the scale of a level eyeHeight , crouchedEyeHeight 1.7, 1.05 m crouching costs speed and the sprint stepHeight 0.45 m a stair and a low plinth, not a crate — a crate you could walk onto is not cover gravity 20 m/s² about twice real; at 9.8 a first-person fall floats jumpSpeed 6.3 m/s clears about a meter: a low wall, not a crate coyoteTime , jumpBufferTime 0.1, 0.12 s a jump just off a ledge, or just before landing, is not eaten slideDuration 1.15 s crouching mid-sprint carries the momentum Names say what a number is — a speed, a duration, an angle — and its unit is in its documentation: meters, seconds and radians throughout. Crouch, slide and room to stand Crouching mid"}, {"slug": "attachments", "title": "Hanging things off joints", "section": "Characters", "headings": ["It is parenting, not a scene graph", "Building a hierarchy", "Attaching to a joint", "Strip the scale", "A grip is a number you set by looking", "Volume: an extent with no mesh", "What it does not do"], "text": "Some things are placed relative to other things: a lamp in a hand, a turret on a hull, a sight on a rifle. TransformHierarchy resolves one transform relative to another, in the right order, every frame. It owns no objects — it is a list of attachments and a list of matrices — so the same hierarchy can place a drawable, a light and a collision volume without knowing any of them exist. It is parenting, not a scene graph A scene graph is an object model — nodes that own children and components, looked up by name. Windage Engine has not made that decision. What a game runs into first is much smaller, and this is that. The same goes for what gets drawn. A SceneObject is a flat pair — an MTKMesh and a Volume — made with SceneObject(loaded:transform:) , which composes the mesh's nodeTransform in for you. There is no parent pointer on it. Building a hierarchy Each node says what it hangs off, as a TransformHierarchy.Attachment : Case Its parent --- --- .world nothing: its local transform is its world transform .node(Int) another node of this hierarchy, which must come earlier .external(Int) something the hierarchy does not own — a joint of a posed character, a platform driven by another system Move a node with setLocalTransform(_:ofNode:) , or replace them all with setLocalTransforms(_:) , then call refresh(attachedTo:) again. localTransforms is read-only, so an array of the wrong length can't slip in: setLocalTransforms(_:) throws for one, and an index past the end stops at once. worldTransforms holds every result. Every structural mistake — a count mismatch, a node attached to itself, a parent after its child, an unknown node or external — throws a TransformHierarchyError when the hierarchy is built, because later it would only be a transform that is quietly wrong. Attaching to a joint A rig is already a hierarchy, resolved by SkeletonPose ). What it cannot do is reach outside itself, and a thing in a hand is exactly that: its parent is a joint, inside a pose, placed by a character's world transform. .external(_:) handles it. The node keeps the grip, authored once , as its local transform, and takes its parent's world transform from the array you hand to refresh(attachedTo:) . How many externals there will be is declared up front, so an out-of-range index is a load-time error rather than an object drawn at the origin in a frame nobody saw. The sample's figures each carry a lantern. Once, at load, it finds a joint and builds one node per figure: Each frame, after posing the figures , it hands over each hand's world transform and places the lanterns: The same matrix moves the drawable, its volume and its light. Placed from last step's pose, hand and lantern would be a frame apart. Strip the scale A child inherits its parent's scale. That is right for a limb and wrong for a held object: a rig authored in centimeters carries a hundredfold scale down to the wrist, and a lantern hung off that matrix is drawn at one percent of its size, in a place that is otherwise correct. (_:).withoutScale removes the scale and keeps rotation and position. It is a function you call, not a flag on the hierarchy, because whether to inherit scale is a decision about what is being attached, and only your game can see that. A mirrored basis stays mirrored. A grip is a number you set by looking A joint's frame is not a promise about where the mesh was drawn. In the sample's figure, a grip worked out from the bind pose sits in the hand with the arm down, and floats above the head with the arm raised, because the drawn limb stops short of where the arithmetic says. Set the grip by looking at the character in the poses it will be in. Volume: an extent with no mesh A Volume is a model-space BoundingBox ( localBounds ) and a transform . It has no opinion about drawing, so something you only collide with or shoot at does not need a GPU mesh to be hit. worldBounds is computed from the transform every time it is read , never stored, so a volume that moves is hit where"}, {"slug": "levels", "title": "Levels and the editor", "section": "World", "headings": ["What a level holds", "A real level", "Things that travel with a placement", "Loading and saving", "LevelEditor"], "text": "A level is a file, not code: things placed in space, points that mean something to the game, the sky, and the lamps. Level reads and writes it as JSON, and LevelEditor is the selection, operations, undo and save that let a game edit its own levels in place, without a window of its own. What a level holds { format} Level is a Codable value type with a format version, a name, and these fields: Field What it is --- --- placements Level.Placement : a mesh name, a position , a yaw in radians, a scale , an anchor , tags , and optionally a light , a surface and a sound markers Level.Marker : a kind , an optional name , a position, an anchor, a yaw and values lights PunctualLight : free-standing lamps sky an optional ProceduralSky ; resolvedSky gives a plausible one when the file has none settings String: Double : numbers your game interprets groundSurface what the ground is made of, for footsteps The engine does not interpret any of the words. A marker's kind , a placement's tags and its surface are your game's vocabulary. The sample reads \"spawn\" and \"objective\" markers and a \"solid\" tag; another game reads its own. The engine never adds a .spawn case, so the format has no opinion about genre. Every field except formatVersion may be left out, so a file written before a field existed still loads. Placements leave out yaw , scale , anchor and tags when they hold their default values, which keeps a large level small. A real level { excerpt} This is trimmed from the sample's Sources/WindageSampleCore/Resources/ruins.json : one spawn marker and one brazier that carries its own lamp and crackle. In the file a lamp's color and intensity are separate, because a person thinks about them separately. In code they are one value, PunctualLight.color , which is the color multiplied by its brightness. A cone is added by giving a direction . innerAngle and outerAngle are in degrees. Things that travel with a placement { carried} A lamp or a sound on a placement is in the placement's own space , so moving, turning, copying or deleting the brazier takes its light and its crackle with it. The lamp's offset scales with the placement. Its range and brightness don't. Before this existed, a brazier and its flame were two entries in one file, and moving the brazier in the editor left its light on the floor. Turn a level into world positions against your game's ground: Level.Anchor says how y is read. With .absolute it is a world height. With .ground it is an offset from the ground under the origin. With .beddedFootprint it is an offset from the lowest ground under the mesh's footprint, which needs footprintCorners on the Ground . Storing an offset instead of a height means a level stays on the ground when the terrain changes. Loading and saving { load} Level.load(named:withExtension:bundle:) reads from a bundle, never from an arbitrary path , so the engine runs the same way sandboxed or not. Level.decode(from:) reads data you already have. Every failure is a LevelError — .notFound , .unreadable , .malformed , or .unsupportedVersion for a file written by a newer format, so a newer file can't load with pieces silently missing. encoded() writes sorted, pretty-printed JSON, so a save that changes nothing produces no diff. A bundle is read-only, so the sample saves edits to its own folder in Application Support. From then on, that copy shadows the shipped file. The sample prints which file it loaded, and ⌘⌫ throws the copy away. Do the same in your game, or someone will edit the shipped file and see nothing change. LevelEditor { editor} LevelEditor wraps a Level and owns everything an edit means. Your game only turns keys and clicks into its calls and draws the result: - Lamps: setLight(_:) gives the selected placement a lamp, replaces it, or removes it with nil . raiseLight(by:) moves it in meters of the world, whatever the placement's scale. brightenLight(by:) works in ratios, and the default configuration.lampBrighten is a third of a stop. - Adding: add(_:at"}, {"slug": "audio", "title": "Audio", "section": "World", "headings": ["What it does and does not do", "Setting it up", "One-shots", "Loops that belong to a place", "Footsteps and landings", "Volume", "Pausing and stopping", "Measuring without listening"], "text": " AudioEngine plays sounds at positions in the world: one-shots for things that happen, and loops for things that keep making noise, like a brazier. Footsteps and landings come from the character controller , which reports what the body did, and from the level , which says what each surface is made of. What it does and does not do { scope} Every sound is spatialized: you give the listener each frame and a world position for each sound. The engine plays one-shots and loops tied to a place. It has no music, no buses and no mixing groups . Those come when a game needs them. Sixteen one-shots can overlap by default ( AudioEngine.Configuration.voiceCount ). Beyond that the oldest voice is reused, because the newest sound is the one that just happened. Setting it up { setup} AudioEngine() throws an AudioEngineError saying why it could not start. load(_:withExtension:bundle:) decodes a sound and keeps it in memory, with wav as the default extension, and throws .soundNotFound or .unreadableSound . bundle has no default on purpose. In an app bundle the sounds are in .main , while in a SwiftPM executable .main holds nothing and the sounds are in .module . load(_:samples:sampleRate:) loads mono samples your game generates. AudioEngine(configuration:) takes the voice count and both spatializers' distance curves ( Configuration.Falloff : reference distance, maximum distance, rolloff). The defaults are the curves described below. Move the listener once a frame: One-shots { one-shots} play(_:at:volume:delay:) plays a sound at a world position. delay is in seconds. At about 343 m/s, an impact 800 m away is heard nearly two and a half seconds after it happens. The delay is counted on the audio clock, so it holds while the engine is paused and stop() cancels a sound still waiting. play(_:volume:) plays without a position, for sounds that happen to the player. Loops that belong to a place { loops} startLoop(_:at:volume:) returns an AudioEngine.Loop handle — keep it, since a loop nobody holds can only be stopped by stopAllLoops() — and loopCount covers the rest. Each loop starts at a different point in its sound, so two braziers playing the same crackle don't sound like one crackle in two places. Loops get their own spatializer with a different falloff. One-shots are tuned for a sound heard across a range: full volume out to 8 m, then 90% of the inverse law. Through that curve, a brazier sounds as loud at 8 m as it does up close, and two of them fill a level with fire. Loops use the physical inverse law from 2 m ( Configuration.loopFalloff ) and measured 1 : 0.250 : 0.100 at 2, 8 and 20 m. A placement can carry a sound in its own space, like its lamp. Level.carriedSounds(on:) gives each one's world position, so a brazier moved in the editor takes its crackle with it. When your level changes, reconcile the loops instead of restarting them . The sample matches loops by name and position first, and only then by placement index, because copying a placement renumbers every placement after it. Footsteps and landings { footsteps} The engine doesn't play footsteps itself. It gives your game the two facts it needs: - What the body did. CharacterController.events lists this step's .stride(foot:speed:) , .landed(impactSpeed:) and .jumped , in order. The list is cleared at the start of every step, so read it after step . - What is underfoot. CharacterController.groundSolid says which solid the feet are on. Level.surface(standingOn:) turns the matching placement into its surface , or the level's groundSurface for nil . Here is the sample's version, trimmed ( variant picks one of three recordings). Footsteps are not positioned, because they happen to the player: A few details are worth knowing: - Strides are counted from the distance the feet actually covered , not the distance asked for. A player pushing into a wall doesn't march on the spot. - Strides are longer than a person's because the speeds are faster. walkStride , sprintStride and crouchStride (2.6, 5 "}, {"slug": "input", "title": "Input and settings", "section": "World", "headings": ["Feeding it events", "Reading it", "Your game's own actions", "Rebinding keys", "Rebinding controller buttons", "PlayerSettings", "Dead zones and the stick curve", "What it does not do"], "text": " InputState turns keyboard, mouse and controller into axes, actions and intents — movement , consumeLook() , lookRate , isHeld(_:) — so nothing downstream reads key codes. InputBindings and PadBindings say which key and button does what, for the engine's actions and your game's own; PlayerSettings holds those and everything else a player sets once. Feeding it events Call start() once, when your window opens — it listens for controllers connecting. Then forward the window's events. Your game gets first refusal on each key: The view makes the KeyCode from the event: KeyCode(rawValue: event.keyCode) . In your own code, name keys by their statics — .w , .space , .leftShift , .digit1 — or by KeyCode(named:) for a name read from a file. Forward flagsChanged . macOS sends Shift, Control, Option and Command only as modifier flags, so a window forwarding keyDown and keyUp alone has no Shift at all. Through flagsChanged(key:modifierFlags:) a modifier is a key like any other, and can be bound. The mouse goes in with addMouseDelta(x:y:) (counted only while setMouseCaptured(true) ), setLeftMouse(_:) and setRightMouse(_:) ; scroll with addScroll(_:) , out as whole notches from consumeScroll() . Call releaseAll() when the window loses focus, or a held key stays held forever, and stop() when it closes. Reading it Once a frame, call pollFrame() — it finds controller presses — and take the mouse once, since it is a displacement that already happened. It comes out already through the player's sensitivity and invert: The stick is a rate, so it goes in every step: The body reads the engine's actions — see CharacterController : Read What it is --- --- movement a MovementInput : forward and strafe from keys and the left stick, clamped to −1…1, and isSprinting isJumpHeld Space, or the bottom face button — held state; the controller finds the press isCrouching a latch: press to crouch, press to stand. standUp() ends it consumeSteps() , consumeNext() the arrows and d-pad as discrete steps, and Tab as a generic \"next\" A sprint press while crouched stands the player up. On a pad, the stick click latches sprint — that thumb is steering — and lets go when the player stops pushing forward; Shift stays held. A game that gives the stick click a second job sets padSprintLatches = false while it applies. Your game's own actions The engine acts on walking, jumping, crouching and sprinting, and nothing else. Fire, aim, reload, use — every verb your game has — is an InputAction you name , bound and read like the engine's: Read What it is --- --- isHeld(_:) the action's key or pad button is down; isKeyHeld(_:) and isPadHeld(_:) for one device consumePresses(of:) presses since you last asked — once a step; a toggle flips on an odd count isLeftMouseHeld , isRightMouseHeld the mouse buttons, as the window reported them leftTrigger , rightTrigger how far each trigger is pulled, 0…1 — how far is far enough is yours The action's name is what a settings file stores, so keep it stable; what a settings screen calls it is your game's to say. Rebinding keys InputBindings maps each action to one KeyCode . A KeyCode is a key position, not a letter, so movement stays under the left hand on any layout; its name is the US legend. standard binds the engine's seven actions; actions lists every one, in the order a settings screen shows them. The rules: - A key in use is swapped, not stolen , so every action still has one. - Escape, Tab and the arrows are refused ( InputBindings.fixed ). Escape is the way out. - Pass your game's own keys in reserving: — the sample reserves P, R, N, M and O, so jump on P cannot pause the game. Rebinding controller buttons PadBindings does the same for the actions that are buttons — the engine's jump, crouch and sprint, and any your game adds. A PadButton is a position — south , east , west , north , the shoulders, the stick clicks — and name(on:) asks the connected pad what it prints there. By default south jumps, east crouches and the left stick click"}, {"slug": "saving", "title": "Saving", "section": "World", "headings": ["SaveStore", "Where files go", "Atomic writes", "A bad file says so", "PlayerSettings", "Versioning saved data", "What it does not do"], "text": " SaveStore keeps what your game remembers between runs. It stores any Codable value as a JSON file, atomically, in the one place a Mac app can always write, sandboxed or not. PlayerSettings is the engine's share of a settings screen, ready to save with it. SaveStore { savestore} SaveStore is a small value type: Member What it does --- --- init(container:name:) container is the folder for your game; name is the file, without extension url where the file lives; only a path, so asking for it creates nothing load() throws - Value? the saved value, nil when nothing has been saved, and an error when a file is there and will not read save(_:) throws writes the value, replacing what was there, and makes the folder the first time clear() throws removes the file; clearing nothing is not an error Name the container after your game , so two games on one machine do not collide and a person can find your files. Use one store per kind of thing: the sample keeps settings and progress apart, so resetting progress does not reset a player's keys. Where files go { where} The file is / .json in the user's Application Support folder: - Unsandboxed, that is ~/Library/Application Support/ / . - Sandboxed, macOS gives the app its own Application Support inside its container, and the same code resolves to it. This is why SaveStore is in the engine at all. The engine never assumes a writable location outside the app's container , because your game, not the engine, is what gets distributed, and it may be sandboxed. A path beside the executable works right up until the app is sandboxed, so there is one place that knows where a game may write. Resources you ship are read from bundles. The save itself is pretty-printed JSON with sorted keys. Atomic writes { atomic} save writes to a temporary file and renames it over the old one, and a rename is atomic. If the game crashes mid-write, the save is either the old one or the new one, never half of either. A bad file says so { bad-file} load() returns nil when there is no file, and throws when there is one that is truncated or written by a build that cannot be decoded. A save that will not load must still not stop the game starting — losing a high score is a disappointment; refusing to launch is a bug report — so catch it, log it, and start fresh. (try? store.load()) ?? fallback is the short form. Starting fresh is the right failure for progress and a bad one for settings, where it means every setting reset. The fix is in the type you save, not in SaveStore ; see versioning below. In tests , use a container of your own and clear what you write: tests sharing the real file clobber a player's save, and parallel tests clobber each other. PlayerSettings { settings} PlayerSettings is what a player sets once and expects to find set again. It is Codable , so it saves through a SaveStore as it is: Property Default Range --- --- --- bindings InputBindings.standard which key does what padBindings PadBindings.standard which controller button does what mouseSensitivity , stickSensitivity 1 PlayerSettings.sensitivityRange , 0.1…5 invertMouse , invertStick false moveDeadzone , lookDeadzone StickResponse.moveDeadzone , .lookDeadzone PlayerSettings.deadzoneRange , 0…0.5 headBobAmount 1 0…1; 0 is off — stored as headBob volume 0.8 0…1 ambienceVolume 1 0…1, under volume brightness 0 PlayerSettings.brightnessRange , −2…2 stops Every numeric setting is clamped to its range when set, and a value that is not finite falls back to its default. mouseLookScale and stickLookScale give the multipliers to apply before Camera.look(mouseDelta:) and Camera.turn(rate:deltaTime:) . See input for bindings and the rules for rebinding. A game with more settings, such as a field of view, a difficulty or subtitles, keeps them beside PlayerSettings rather than inside it. Versioning saved data { versioning} SaveStore does not version what it stores; the type you save does. Two patterns, both in the engine and its sample: Decode field by field. PlayerSet"}, {"slug": "splash", "title": "The splash", "section": "Your game's name", "headings": ["What it shows", "Using it", "Timing", "Appearance", "Your game's name", "In the sample"], "text": " SplashScreen is the engine saying its own name: the engine's mark and \"Windage Engine\" over a dark ground, fading up, holding and fading away. A game that wants to credit the engine at launch has nothing to build: no art, no texture and no pipeline, because it is drawn with the HUD . Nothing runs unless your game runs it. What it shows { lockup} One line across: an optional lead , the mark, and the name, with \"WINDAGE\" large over \"ENGINE\" small and tracked out. With lead: \"MADE WITH\" , as the sample uses, the lead sits in small capitals to the left of the mark, one word per line. The name is set in the HUD's font rather than drawn: the engine has a drawn mark but not yet a drawn wordmark. There is no version on it by default. showsVersion exists for development builds and is off, because no shipped game shows its engine's version. Using it { use} It is an overlay and a clock, not a mode. Advance it from your simulation step, draw it from your HUD , and drop it when it is done. From the sample: Three rules come with it: - Keep stepping behind it. The game is usually loading during a splash. A game that suspends its step while the splash is up makes its first second unrepeatable. - skip() is a side effect of a press, never a consumer of one. A splash that eats the first keystroke eats the first keystroke of whatever the player was already doing. The sample checks this with a scripted walk run under the splash, which must end exactly where the same walk ends without it. See walks . - Drop it when isFinished is true. An overlay at zero opacity still costs its vertices. Draw it last in encodeHUD , so it covers the game's own interface. Timing { timing} Property Default Meaning --- --- --- fadeIn 0.4 s fading up from nothing hold 1.6 s at full strength fadeOut 0.5 s fading away skipDelay 0.25 s presses ignored after it appears duration is the three together. elapsed , opacity (0 to 1), isSkipped and isFinished report where it is. A skip runs it to the start of its fade, not to its end. A splash that vanishes on the frame a key goes down looks like a dropped frame; one that fades out looks intended. A skip is ignored for the first skipDelay seconds, because the key or click that launched the game is often still down when the first frame appears, and ignored again once it is fading, so holding a key does not cut the fade short twice. The fades are linear ramps in elapsed , which is a sum of step lengths, so the picture at a given moment is the same at 30 steps a second and at 240. The tests check exactly that. Appearance { appearance} Property Default --- --- background InterfaceColor(hex: 0x0B0D10) , near-black so it reads as a surface foreground EngineMark.display , the light tone for dark grounds lead nil , no lead; the mark and name center on their own markScale 0.12 of the viewport's width; everything else is sized from the mark showsVersion false All are var s, set after SplashScreen(lead:) . If you set showsVersion , the text drawn is Engine.version . Your game's name { your-name} The splash says the engine's name, not yours. It reads Engine.name , which is \"Windage Engine\" , and there is no setting to put anything else in it. It is the engine's credit; the only words you choose are the lead . Your game's own title card, logo or studio card is yours to draw, with the same tools. The HUD draws text, rectangles and polygons in exact colors, so a title screen needs nothing from the engine beyond that. Show it before or after the engine's splash as you like; SplashScreen does not install itself in the frame loop or take control of what comes next. Where the engine's name appears without a splash — a credits screen, an about box, a corner — draw the mark with EngineMark.draw(into:origin:height:color:) and set Engine.name beside it. The sample also puts the engine's name and version in its window title: The splash is optional. What a game must show is a credits line — \"Uses Windage Engine © 2026 Pixla LLC\" in its credits or legal sc"}, {"slug": "mark", "title": "The mark", "section": "Your game's name", "headings": ["What it is a picture of", "Drawing it", "The outline", "Two tones, no hue", "The files in Brand/", "Crediting the engine"], "text": " EngineMark is the engine's mark, the Drift W, as geometry. Draw it into your HUD at any size to credit the engine in a corner, a credits screen or an about box; the splash uses it the same way. The SVG files in Brand/ are written from the same geometry, so there is one drawing of the mark, not several that drift apart. What it is a picture of { drawing} Windage is the sideways carry of a shot, and the correction dialed against it. The mark is a W drawn as one continuous stroke whose right half is carried up by 16% of its own height . The middle apex sits well below both shoulders, which is what makes it read as a W rather than two Vs; the lift is the windage. The mark is square: EngineMark.width(forHeight:) returns the height. Drawing it { draw} draw(into:origin:height:color:) fills the mark with its top-left corner at origin in the HUD's coordinates. It needs no texture, no resource and no pipeline of its own: it is a call to HUDRenderer.fillPolygon(_:color:feather:) , with the HUD's default one-unit feather so its diagonals are not stair-stepped. A height of 0, or a color with zero alpha, draws nothing. The outline EngineMark.outline is the shape itself: thirteen points in a unit square, origin at the top left, y downward , the same as HUDRenderer , in order around the letter starting at the left shoulder. Scale and offset it yourself if you want the mark in something other than the HUD: It is constructed, not traced: five points on the stroke's center line (left shoulder, left valley, middle apex, right valley, right shoulder) and a stroke width measured across, as type is. Each arm's edges are its center line moved half the width either way, every corner is where two of those edges meet, and every end is cut flat. The tests check that it stays inside its square, never crosses itself, has its apex cut flat and well below the shoulders, and that only its right half is lifted. Two tones, no hue { tones} Tone Value For --- --- --- EngineMark.display F2F4F7 dark grounds: a game, a splash, a store capsule EngineMark.deep 1B2027 light grounds: a page, print, anything that must meet a contrast ratio Both are InterfaceColor s, drawn exactly as authored because the HUD draws after tone mapping. The mark has no hue on purpose. It appears inside somebody else's game, where the game's own colors should lead. A hue of its own would be a second brand competing with the one the player came for. You may pass any InterfaceColor to draw , but the two tones are the mark as intended. The files in Brand/ { files} EngineMarkTests writes every SVG of the mark from EngineMark.outline , and fails if a file on disk and the outline disagree: File What it is --- --- windage-engine.svg the mark in deep , for light grounds windage-engine-display.svg the mark in display , for dark grounds windage-engine-icon.svg an app icon on a 1024 canvas: an 824 tile inset 100, the mark at 56% favicon.svg a full-bleed tile with the mark at 76%, for a browser tab Each file carries the engine's name as its title. Brand/exports/ holds PNGs of all four at the sizes a site, a store page and a Dock need, rasterized from the SVGs by Tools/make_brand_exports.sh (which needs rsvg-convert ; it is a brand tool, not part of the build). Changing the mark means changing the construction in EngineMark.swift , never a file: The last line re-records the splash's frame, which keeps a picture of the mark on screen with a tolerance of its own, because a lit scene's tolerance could not see a mark move. See frames . Crediting the engine { credit} Required: a credits line. A game that ships the engine names it in its credits or legal screen — for example \"Uses Windage Engine © 2026 Pixla LLC\" — with where the license can be read (PolyForm Small Business 1.0.0, whose Required Notice: line this is). That is the whole requirement, as it is for Unreal and Godot. A splash is not required. Showing the engine's name or mark more visibly is your choice; ways to do it, from most to least visible: 1"}, {"slug": "walks", "title": "Scripted walks", "section": "Checking your game", "headings": ["The script", "The log", "Checking every walk", "The same approach in your game"], "text": "A scripted walk plays the game from a line of keys and seconds and logs where the feet were at the end of each segment. Unlike \"it felt right on the stairs\", anyone can repeat it, and Tools/walks.py turns the walks into a check that fails when a change moves where they end. This is the sample's tooling, not engine API. The script reader, the log line and Tools/walks.py belong to WindageSample . The engine provides the pieces they are built from, listed at the end ( your-game), and a game that wants the same check builds its own reader on top of them. The script { syntax} Set WINDAGE_SAMPLE_WALK to segments separated by spaces. Each segment is keys:seconds : Segment What it does --- --- w:1.0 holds W for one second, then releases it w+shift:0.8 holds several keys at once, joined with + space:0.05 any name KeyCode(named:) knows: letters, space , shift , up , down , left , right wait:1.2 holds nothing click:0.1 starts with a mouse click yaw:90 , pitch:-40 turns the view to that many degrees, and takes no time mousex:500 , mousey:-80 moves the mouse that many points, through the real mouse path pad.south:0.1 presses a controller element pad.lookRight@0.2:1 holds a stick a fifth of the way over The pad elements are south , east , west , north , leftShoulder , rightShoulder , leftStick , rightStick and menu . The d-pad is up , down , left and right , the look stick is lookUp , lookDown , lookLeft and lookRight , and the move stick is moveUp , moveDown , moveLeft and moveRight . A script that names any of them gets a virtual pad for its whole run. The keys go through the game, not around it : the same keyPressed a keyboard calls, so the bindings, the settings screen and the editor all see them. Segments are whole simulation steps. Each boundary is measured from the start of the script and lands on the nearest step, so roundings can't add up over a long script. A walk that must hold at several step rates uses whole steps of the slowest one, which means multiples of 1/30 s. The log { log} When a segment ends, the sample prints where the feet are, the highest they got during the segment, whether they're on the ground, and how high the eye is above them. Indented lines after it describe the same segment. Here is the walk above going up the ruins' stairs: The indented lines can also report a landing's impact speed and how far the eye dipped. A segment that turned the view prints look '…' → view turned …° right, …° up . Checking every walk { check} Tools/walks.json lists the walks the README and the handoff document, each with what it measured. Tools/walks.py runs them all: Each walk runs as a check ( WINDAGE_SAMPLE_CHECK= ), with no window, no keyboard, no sound and its own saves, so it runs while you work and a player's rebinding can't make it fail. Four run at a time. A result depends on the step count, not the clock, so running them together only changes how long the run takes. A walk entry names its level and script , and each item under expect says which segment it checks ( after , with an optional occurrence ) and what to compare: - feet and highest must be within the file's tolerance of 2 cm , plus half a centimeter for the log's rounding. - footsteps and the surfaces they were on must match exactly. - bob may differ by a millimeter, a landed speed by a tenth of a meter per second, and the eye's dip not at all. - look checks a view turn, and line matches any log line with a regular expression. 2 cm is wide enough for a real change to hide in, so --record is there for looking. A recorded number changes only on purpose, with its reason in the CHANGELOG. like reuses another walk's script and expectations. \"crate behind the splash\" uses it to check that the engine's splash takes nothing from the walk underneath. rates runs a walk at several step rates ( WINDAGE_SAMPLE_RATE ). The course walk runs at 30, 60, 120 and 240 steps a second and must give the same numbers at each. The same approach in your game { your-game} The engine does not"}, {"slug": "frames", "title": "Recorded frames", "section": "Checking your game", "headings": ["Drawing a frame into a texture", "The same picture every time", "Checking every frame", "Every shadow was a frame stale", "A check is only a check if it fails"], "text": "A renderer can only be judged by looking at it, and a screenshot someone looked at once protects nothing afterwards. FrameRenderer.render(into:) draws a frame into a texture of your own, with no window. The sample's Tools/frames.py uses it to draw a set of recorded viewpoints and compare each one, pixel by pixel, against the frame kept for it. That makes shadows, the tone map and the lights something a check can fail on. Drawing a frame into a texture { render-into} It runs the same passes in the same order as a displayed frame: the pre-passes, the scene into its HDR target, then the tone map and the interface. What comes out is what a player would have seen. It waits for the GPU before returning, so you can read the texture straight away. The texture needs FrameRenderer.colorPixelFormat and .renderTarget . To read it back, it also needs shared or managed storage, and like everything else the GPU reads, it has to be in the residency set. This is the sample's version, trimmed: The bytes are BGRA. The sample swaps them to RGBA and writes a PNG with NSBitmapImageRep . Comparing frames is only one use: a thumbnail or a photo a game saves also wants the frame the game draws, without a drawable to draw it into. The same picture every time { deterministic} A comparison only means something if both runs draw the same frame. The sample makes sure of that through environment variables: - WINDAGE_SAMPLE_FRAME= draws one frame to a PNG and quits. The window is never shown, and nothing has been simulated yet, so the clock is full, no crate has been collected, and the lifts and turntable are where the level puts them. - WINDAGE_SAMPLE_LEVEL and WINDAGE_SAMPLE_CAMERA=x,y,z,yaw,pitch choose the viewpoint, with angles in degrees. A camera placed this way flies, because a body under a raised camera would fall to the ground before the first frame. - The engine's splash is off for these frames unless one asks for it, because a splash over every frame would test the splash and never the scene. Checking every frame { check} Tools/frames.json lists the viewpoints: the ruins from above, the lintel at eye height, the courtyard, the turntable, and the splash. Each has a level , a camera and a shows line saying what it is there to guard. The recorded frames are in Tools/frames/ . A frame fails when more than 0.5% of its pixels differ by more than 5 in any channel. That allows a GPU its last bit but doesn't let a shadow move. A frame can set a tighter tolerance of its own, and the splash has to. It is a small flat drawing, where moving the mark's curve by 4% of its height changes a quarter of a percent of the frame. That is well inside the scene's half percent, so the splash checks with a channel tolerance of 2 and no differing pixels at all. After --record , look at the new frames before committing them. A recorded frame should change when the renderer changes, and the reason goes in the CHANGELOG. What the record guards is that someone looked. Every shadow was a frame stale { stale-shadows} The first frame drawn this way found a bug that had been there all along. Metal 4 tracks no hazards between passes. The scene pass sampled shadow maps that an earlier pass in the same command buffer was still writing, and nothing told the GPU to wait. In play it looked right, because the previous frame's shadow maps were there to sample. Every shadow was simply one frame stale. The very first frame had nothing in those maps yet, and it came out differently on every run: once as a black scene, once blotched in cluster-sized squares. Two consumer barriers fixed it, one making the scene wait for the shadows and one making the tone map wait for the scene. Since then, four runs of the same frame produce the same bytes. A game can't see this bug, and a test that draws no pixels can't either. A frame drawn twice and compared shows it. A check is only a check if it fails { fails} Each frame was tested against a deliberate break. Switching the sample's tone map from ACES to "}, {"slug": "changelog", "title": "Changelog", "section": "", "headings": ["1.0.0 — 2026-10-14", "Added"], "text": " 1.0.0 — 2026-10-14 { v1-0-0} Initial release. A Swift 6 and Metal 4 game engine for the Mac, extracted from working games. Added - Frame pacing: frames in flight, a fixed simulation step, and a clock that keeps time with the window hidden. GameLoop , FrameRenderer , FixedTimestep . - Rendering: reversed-Z depth, an HDR scene with tone mapping and exposure in stops, physically based materials (base color, normal, occlusion–roughness–metallic and emission maps), and image-based lighting from the sky. - Light and shadow: cascaded sun shadows, and spot and point lights binned into clusters, each able to cast. ShadowMap , ClusteredLights . - Meshes: loading through Model I/O, with USD's orientation and units handled. MeshLoader . - Many copies: instanced drawing, one draw per kind of prop. InstanceBatches , InstanceBuffer . - Detail by distance: simpler tiers of every mesh made when it loads, and the tier a copy's distance earns. MeshDetail , using meshoptimizer's simplifier (MIT). - Culling: frustum culling for the screen and for each shadow cascade. Frustum . - Characters: skinned animation from USD, cross-fades, and things hung off joints. SkeletonPose , TransformHierarchy . - A body with feet: stairs, slopes, lifts, turntables and being pushed, with the same result at any step rate. CharacterController . - Particles: sparks, embers and dust, simulated on the fixed step and drawn in one instanced draw. ParticleSystem . - Levels: a versioned level format, and an editor you use inside your running game. Level , LevelEditor . - Sound: positional audio and looping ambience that belongs to a place. AudioEngine . - Input and settings: keys and controllers a player rebinds, per-device sensitivity, dead zones and invert, and settings that save. InputBindings , SaveStore . - The HUD: text and shapes drawn after the tone map. HUDRenderer . - Your game's name: a splash screen and the engine's mark. SplashScreen , EngineMark . - Checks: scripted walks and recorded frames, so a change that moves the player or the picture fails a check instead of going unnoticed. Tools/walks.py , Tools/frames.py . - A sample game built only against the public API. swift run WindageSample . "}, {"slug": "reference", "title": "Reference", "section": "", "headings": ["Types"], "text": "Every public type in the WindageEngine module, with the first line of its documentation and the guide that explains it. Types { types} Type Kind What it is Guide --- --- --- --- AnimationBinding struct Which of a rig's joints each of a clip's joints drives, worked out once. Skeletons and clips AnimationClip struct A named stretch of animation: what each joint does, and for how long. Skeletons and clips AnimationClipError enum Why a JointTrack or an AnimationClip could not be made from the data it was given. — AnimationPlayhead struct Where a character is in a clip: the clip, its mapping onto a rig, and the time. Skeletons and clips AssetOrientation struct What space an asset was authored in, and the one matrix that brings it into this engine's. Loading meshes AudioEngine class Positional one-shot audio. Audio AudioEngineError enum Why an AudioEngine could not be made, or a sound could not be loaded into one. — BindingOutcome enum What putting an action on a key or a button did — see InputBindings/bind(_:to:reserving:) and PadBindings/bind(_:to:reserving:) . — BoundingBox struct An axis-aligned box, and the ray test that goes with it. Culling Camera struct A first-person camera, in meters. Camera CharacterController struct A body with feet: walking across the ground, standing on things, stepping up, falling off, and crouching. CharacterController ClusteredLights struct Which lights reach each cluster, as the two arrays a shader reads. Clustered lights ClusterGrid struct The view frustum cut into a grid of cells, so a fragment can ask which lights reach it rather than inheriting a list chosen for the whole mesh it belongs to. Clustered lights Engine enum Identifying information for the engine itself. Your first game EngineMark enum The engine's own mark: the Drift W — the initial, with the windage dialled in. The mark EnvironmentMap class The lighting an environment contributes: what it looks like reflected, and what it looks like scattered. Sky and environment EnvironmentMapError enum Why an EnvironmentMap could not be made: which of its two cube textures the device would not create. — FixedTimestep struct Advances simulation in fixed increments, independently of how often — or how irregularly — frames arrive. Fixed timestep FontAtlas class A rasterized glyph sheet, built at launch from an installed font. The HUD FontAtlasError enum Why a FontAtlas could not be built. The HUD FrameRenderer class Drives frames, owns the Metal 4 objects every renderer needs, and enforces the engine's reversed-Z depth convention. GameLoop FrameRendererError enum Why a FrameRenderer could not be made. GameLoop Frustum struct The planes of a view-projection, and whether a box is worth drawing. Culling GameLoop protocol What a game supplies to the engine's frame loop. GameLoop GLTFError enum Why a glTF file could not be read. — HUDRenderer class — The HUD HUDRendererError enum Draws the interface, after the scene has been tone mapped. The HUD ImportedMaterial struct How a surface looks: glTF 2.0's metallic-roughness model, which USD's UsdPreviewSurface also speaks. — ImportedMesh struct One mesh: its vertices, where it sits, and its triangles grouped by material. — ImportedModel struct A model read from a file, as plain values: meshes, the materials they use, and their images. — ImportedSubmesh struct Triangles drawn with one material: three indices into the mesh's vertices per triangle. — ImportedTexture class An image, kept both as the file it came from and as pixels. — ImportedTextureReference struct A texture as a material uses it: the image, how it is sampled, and how its coordinates are moved. — InputAction struct Something a player does, which a key or a pad button can be bound to. — InputBindings struct Which key does what, and the rules for changing it. Input and settings InputState class Collects keyboard, mouse and gamepad into something the simulation can read. Input and settings InstanceBatches struct Many copies of the same meshes, grou"}];
